WEBVTT

00:02:38.890 --> 00:02:40.430
- This is very kind of you.

00:02:40.430 --> 00:02:54.330
- It's a privilege.

00:02:54.330 --> 00:02:55.380
- Where are you going?

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:57.420
- Just over there, the mutual building.

00:02:57.420 --> 00:02:58.890
My doctor is in there.

00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:00.000
You see, I have hope

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:01.000
Fine.

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:01.830
Well, I'm all right now.

00:03:01.830 --> 00:03:02.830
Good luck to you.

00:03:02.830 --> 00:03:03.830
Thank you.

00:03:03.830 --> 00:03:04.830
Thank you so much.

00:03:04.830 --> 00:03:05.830
Merry Christmas to you.

00:03:05.830 --> 00:03:07.200
Merry Christmas.

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:40.830
Come on now, darling, we have to go.

00:03:44.110 --> 00:03:52.480
Oh, you saved her.

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:54.840
Oh, thank God you saved my baby.

00:03:55.220 --> 00:03:56.590
Oh, how can I ever thank you?

00:03:56.880 --> 00:03:58.610
Don't try. Just don't let it happen again.

00:03:58.750 --> 00:04:00.000
I won't. I promise you.

00:04:00.030 --> 00:04:02.030
- I won't. - All right, remember that. Now, on your way.

00:04:02.030 --> 00:04:04.800
Yes.

00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:53.760
On your red foot, botanical edge is similar.

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:55.620
Pay me heed, magenta.

00:04:55.880 --> 00:04:59.240
This is a specimen of the white fur, the ameot cancola.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:04.320
- Surely you, being a native Roman, know your Latin. - You want to buy or not?

00:05:06.620 --> 00:05:10.360
- Well, if it isn't, my dear, beautiful Jill. - Oh, hello, Professor.

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:14.470
- Well, what are you doing in this disreputable part of town? - I'm buying a Christmas tree.

00:05:14.490 --> 00:05:16.970
- Hello, Mr. Majeni. - Oh, Mrs. Abraham, how do you go?

00:05:17.050 --> 00:05:20.930
- How much do you charge for this miserable weed? - $1.85.

00:05:21.010 --> 00:05:24.600
$1.85 for this half-hearted twig?

00:05:24.770 --> 00:05:28.520
I shall pay you 10 cents a branch or take my trade elsewhere.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:33.460
- What can I do for you, Mrs. Rohan? - You can save me that tree outside.

00:05:33.540 --> 00:05:35.920
- The big one right by the door. - Okay.

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:36.980
Thank you.

00:05:37.120 --> 00:05:39.340
Every Christmas for the past 18 years,

00:05:39.420 --> 00:05:42.340
Magenti and I have been re-enacting the same argument.

00:05:42.420 --> 00:05:44.440
I didn't know you celebrated Christmas.

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:46.190
I thought you had no religion.

00:05:46.270 --> 00:05:47.850
Well, that's true, my dear.

00:05:47.930 --> 00:05:51.400
But I like to have a Christmas tree because it reminds me of my childhood.

00:05:51.850 --> 00:05:56.070
I feel for some reason that this is a good time of year for looking backwards.

00:05:56.730 --> 00:05:59.160
Can you imagine me ever having been a child?

00:06:00.350 --> 00:06:05.630
- How's Henry? I haven't seen him in quite some time. - Well, he's well, thank you.

00:06:06.700 --> 00:06:08.950
He's terribly tired and worried.

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:12.520
Imagine he's having difficulty raising money for the cathedral.

00:06:14.360 --> 00:06:18.550
It's slow work. How's your book coming?

00:06:18.760 --> 00:06:22.370
Oh, splendidly. Greatest history of Rome since Gibbon.

00:06:22.750 --> 00:06:24.430
But, of course, nobody will read it.

00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:29.830
And now, my good man, I do not choose to prolong this tawdry bickering any further.

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:37.410
Ten cents a branch. It's a dollar and forty.

00:06:37.490 --> 00:06:40.900
Very well, my venal friend. Here is your blood money.

00:06:41.010 --> 00:06:44.020
Mr. Magenta, will you send the tree up on Christmas Eve?

00:06:44.020 --> 00:06:47.090
- But not until late. I don't want my daughter to see. - Sure, don't you worry.

00:06:47.170 --> 00:06:49.700
I send it when the little bambina goes to bed.

00:06:49.790 --> 00:06:53.520
- Merry Christmas. - Merry Christmas!

00:07:02.430 --> 00:07:06.350
There's something I'd like you to give Henry with my compliments for his cathedral fund.

00:07:06.350 --> 00:07:08.610
That has been my lucky piece.

00:07:08.610 --> 00:07:11.830
Not that it's ever brought me luck, except knowing you.

00:07:11.830 --> 00:07:12.950
It's an old Roman coin.

00:07:12.950 --> 00:07:15.950
I picked it up years ago in a junk shop in Brindisi.

00:07:15.950 --> 00:07:18.560
It has little value.

00:07:18.560 --> 00:07:19.560
It's a wonderful contribution.

00:07:19.560 --> 00:07:20.560
Nonsense.

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:26.040
It might be called the widow's mite, if it weren't for the fact that I'm not a widow.

00:07:26.040 --> 00:07:30.000
Junior, this is no occasion.

00:07:31.950 --> 00:07:32.850
Stop snowing it.

00:07:34.450 --> 00:07:36.980
If only we could spend Christmas back here

00:07:36.980 --> 00:07:39.760
where we were so happy with you and all of our old friends.

00:07:39.820 --> 00:07:40.560
No, no, no.

00:07:41.410 --> 00:07:44.790
Good night, Professor.

00:07:45.230 --> 00:07:47.000
I'll see you again very soon.

00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:48.260
You can't be soon enough.

00:07:48.840 --> 00:07:49.480
Be mad, Junior.

00:07:51.640 --> 00:08:00.000
Yes, sir.

00:08:00.030 --> 00:08:02.590
How fine to see you again after all these years.

00:08:02.590 --> 00:08:04.750
- How well you look. - Yes, how are you?

00:08:04.750 --> 00:08:07.290
- Never better, and you? - Oh, quite well also, thank you.

00:08:10.090 --> 00:08:12.510
- I don't think you remember me. - Oh, that's preposterous.

00:08:12.510 --> 00:08:14.310
Of course I do.

00:08:14.310 --> 00:08:17.390
- Where did we meet? - Now, Professor, after all these years...

00:08:17.390 --> 00:08:19.030
Well, not just a moment.

00:08:19.030 --> 00:08:20.410
It wasn't Vienna, was it?

00:08:20.410 --> 00:08:22.910
- Vienna, beautiful old Vienna. - The university.

00:08:22.910 --> 00:08:23.790
The university.

00:08:23.790 --> 00:08:25.680
When I was lecturing there on Roman history.

00:08:25.680 --> 00:08:28.400
That's it, Professor, and what great lectures they were.

00:08:28.400 --> 00:08:30.000
And what a one you were with the ladies.

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:31.960
Oh, fancy you're remembering that.

00:08:31.960 --> 00:08:34.010
I must confess I had my moment.

00:08:34.010 --> 00:08:36.010
And still have.

00:08:36.010 --> 00:08:38.010
Where are you going?

00:08:38.010 --> 00:08:41.290
I couldn't help noticing your tender parting from Julia.

00:08:41.290 --> 00:08:43.300
Do you know Julia?

00:08:43.300 --> 00:08:44.300
In a way, yes.

00:08:44.300 --> 00:08:45.300
Poor child.

00:08:45.300 --> 00:08:47.350
She's unhappy.

00:08:47.350 --> 00:08:49.360
When were you in Vienna?

00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:51.360
Oh, I've been there many times.

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:54.850
I'm interested in Julia and Henry.

00:08:54.850 --> 00:08:56.130
What seems to be their trouble?

00:08:56.130 --> 00:08:58.130
I never see Henry anymore.

00:08:58.130 --> 00:09:00.000
He has no time for riffraff life.

00:09:00.990 --> 00:09:03.570
He now consorts with the vulgar rich, like Mrs. Hamilton.

00:09:04.090 --> 00:09:06.830
You know, she had me fired from the university here.

00:09:07.120 --> 00:09:08.310
He said I was a radical.

00:09:08.750 --> 00:09:12.450
I, who have never taken any interest in politics since the death of Nero.

00:09:13.070 --> 00:09:14.080
Look at that.

00:09:15.560 --> 00:09:17.960
Henry's old church, perishing from neglect.

00:09:20.040 --> 00:09:22.640
It's such a nice little church.

00:09:23.030 --> 00:09:23.970
Too little, I'm afraid.

00:09:25.030 --> 00:09:27.230
It can't stand up against the march of progress.

00:09:28.140 --> 00:09:30.000
Well, how does people see on?

00:09:30.220 --> 00:09:32.860
delighted to have seen you again professor it's a pleasure we must get

00:09:32.860 --> 00:09:39.130
together and have a drink to those old days in vienna by all means good evening

00:09:39.130 --> 00:09:44.150
professor oh pat do you any idea who that man is

00:09:44.150 --> 00:10:00.000
oh he's a stranger to me good evening matilda is debbie in bed you

00:10:00.640 --> 00:10:04.640
Yes, ma'am, and Mrs. Hamilton and the committee are in there with the bishop.

00:10:04.640 --> 00:10:07.640
The dinner's been waiting a long time, madame Harris.

00:10:07.640 --> 00:10:09.640
I don't know what to do about it in the kitchen.

00:10:09.640 --> 00:10:12.420
Well, we'll have dinner just as soon as they leave.

00:10:12.420 --> 00:10:14.420
What about the chicken, Mrs. Brown?

00:10:14.420 --> 00:10:16.880
Don't worry about it, Matilda, please, here. Thank you.

00:10:16.880 --> 00:10:19.510
Oh, Matilda, have a bag.

00:10:26.980 --> 00:10:30.000
I'm ready to see the whole antecedent.

00:10:33.360 --> 00:10:37.960
terribly sorry i'm so late good evening mrs hamilton but i was delayed christmas shopping

00:10:37.960 --> 00:10:45.810
good evening mr perry mrs trambeau hello mrs ward i i hope you've been having a good meeting we

00:10:45.810 --> 00:10:51.070
have not i've never in my life encountered such pleasant thinking do you think we've made any

00:10:51.070 --> 00:10:55.280
progress oh no no mrs hamilton mr perry was about to tell us something when my wife came in

00:10:55.280 --> 00:11:00.000
merely a suggestion if mrs hamilton approves we might be able to place the george b

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:02.760
the Hamilton Memorial Chapel here on the North East.

00:11:02.840 --> 00:11:05.500
It would be completely out of sight there. I won't stand for it.

00:11:05.580 --> 00:11:07.880
Mrs. Hamilton, surely you understand that this cathedral

00:11:07.960 --> 00:11:10.060
cannot be designed for the glory of an individual.

00:11:10.140 --> 00:11:12.130
It has to be created for all the people.

00:11:12.300 --> 00:11:14.910
I'm very displeased at your whole attitude, Henry Brown.

00:11:14.990 --> 00:11:17.490
You seem to forget that I was instrumental in having you made bishop,

00:11:17.570 --> 00:11:19.310
although others thought you too young.

00:11:19.470 --> 00:11:21.800
- Is that an exaggeration? - Oh, yes, Miss...

00:11:22.040 --> 00:11:23.500
I mean, no.

00:11:23.590 --> 00:11:25.710
You were the guiding spirit in the whole thing.

00:11:25.790 --> 00:11:28.480
- I distinctly remember that you told us... - I had every confidence in you

00:11:28.560 --> 00:11:30.000
when you were a poor little parson at church

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:32.000
I confess my confidence is weakened.

00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:34.050
I regret I've been such a disappointment.

00:11:34.050 --> 00:11:37.060
Regrets and apologies are no good whatsoever.

00:11:37.060 --> 00:11:39.050
You give me the impression of being confused,

00:11:39.050 --> 00:11:41.050
indecisive and utterly ineffectual.

00:11:41.050 --> 00:11:44.090
That is not the kind of leadership we expect of our bishop.

00:11:44.090 --> 00:11:46.550
You better remember one thing.

00:11:46.550 --> 00:11:48.760
You will build that cathedral as I want it,

00:11:48.760 --> 00:11:50.780
or you will not build it at all.

00:11:50.780 --> 00:11:53.110
That's all I have to say.

00:11:53.110 --> 00:11:56.670
Will someone get this dog out the way?

00:11:56.670 --> 00:11:57.620
Juliet.

00:11:57.620 --> 00:11:58.550
Queen.

00:12:00.830 --> 00:12:04.120
- Good night, Mrs. Hamilton. - Good evening, Mrs. Brown.

00:12:04.260 --> 00:12:08.160
- Good evening, Mrs. Brown. - Mrs. Brown, good day.

00:12:20.220 --> 00:12:22.020
- Can we serve dinner now, ma'am? - Yes, Matilda.

00:12:22.220 --> 00:12:25.320
- Lydia says the chicken will be burnt to a crisp. - We'll be right in.

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:33.000
You knew that Mrs. Hamilton and the others were expected here for the meeting this afternoon.

00:12:33.100 --> 00:12:36.020
I know, Henry. I'm sorry I was late.

00:12:36.120 --> 00:12:38.020
What a ghastly afternoon. What a ghastly woman.

00:12:38.120 --> 00:12:41.580
I trust she understood that I have no intention of being strangled by her purse strings.

00:12:41.680 --> 00:12:43.580
I hope she did, and I was proud of you.

00:12:43.680 --> 00:12:45.560
I had a most unchristian impulse to take those blueprints

00:12:45.660 --> 00:12:48.840
and give her a good whack over the mink coat.

00:12:48.850 --> 00:12:51.430
I thought you stood up to her magnificently.

00:12:51.530 --> 00:12:54.320
I appreciate your appreciation, but what about my cathedral?

00:12:54.420 --> 00:13:00.000
May I make a suggestion, Henry? Why not postpone the cathedral? At least forget about it.

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:02.300
- You don't have to worry about it until after Christmas. - Impossible.

00:13:02.380 --> 00:13:05.710
The House of God can't just be put off. This cathedral must rise.

00:13:05.790 --> 00:13:09.470
Plenty of other rich people in this town, and if I had to enlist their financial enthusiasm,

00:13:09.550 --> 00:13:11.770
then I shall have to take advantage of their Yuletide spirit.

00:13:11.850 --> 00:13:13.910
Oh, I can see it all now.

00:13:13.990 --> 00:13:17.530
The McWhithies, the Horns, the Van Dusens, the luncheons, the committee meetings,

00:13:17.610 --> 00:13:21.370
and you, you there flattering them, kowtowing to them, begging them.

00:13:21.450 --> 00:13:22.810
It's got to be done.

00:13:22.890 --> 00:13:25.930
Oh, Henry, if you could see your poor harassed face.

00:13:26.010 --> 00:13:28.370
Well, you haven't done very much to help it.

00:13:28.450 --> 00:13:30.000
Yes, Miss Cassaway, what is it?

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:32.100
Mr. Traver on the phone, Bishop. He says it's urgent.

00:13:32.160 --> 00:13:33.620
Tell him the Bishop will call him back, please.

00:13:33.900 --> 00:13:34.660
Yes, Mrs. Brown.

00:13:37.080 --> 00:13:39.410
Henry, what's happened to you?

00:13:39.660 --> 00:13:41.730
What's happened to us and our marriage?

00:13:42.140 --> 00:13:44.960
We used to have such fun, you and Debbie and I.

00:13:45.640 --> 00:13:48.000
We used to be happy. We used to make other people happy.

00:13:48.810 --> 00:13:50.090
Oh, Henry, that was your gift.

00:13:50.500 --> 00:13:52.640
You're no financier and you're not a promoter.

00:13:52.780 --> 00:13:54.740
Julia, you can't see beyond the end of your nose.

00:13:54.860 --> 00:13:56.990
I want this cathedral to stand like a great beacon.

00:13:57.110 --> 00:13:58.150
I want its light to shine...

00:13:58.150 --> 00:14:00.000
Oh, never mind, Henry, never mind. Keep that.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:02.590
for your next committee, ma'am.

00:14:07.230 --> 00:14:11.400
- Here's a contribution I collected. - What's that?

00:14:11.660 --> 00:14:14.470
It's an old Roman coin. Professor Wathridge sent it to you.

00:14:14.780 --> 00:14:17.730
- Wasn't it sweet of him? - Old fool, what does he think I can do with that?

00:14:19.380 --> 00:14:24.040
It's beginning, and now all you need is another four million.

00:14:24.120 --> 00:14:25.600
Julia, don't be flippant about this!

00:14:30.510 --> 00:14:33.150
Is dinner ready?

00:14:33.150 --> 00:14:43.820
Let's go in and get it over with. I have a lot of work to do.

00:14:43.820 --> 00:14:49.410
Receive. May the Lord make us truly thankful.

00:14:49.410 --> 00:14:49.970
Amen.

00:15:16.860 --> 00:15:30.000
I was just thinking, tomorrow maybe we could go out together, walk around the way we

00:15:30.030 --> 00:15:33.360
We could go and call on the professor.

00:15:33.360 --> 00:15:36.140
Go to the park and watch the skaters.

00:15:36.140 --> 00:15:38.550
That sort of thing.

00:15:38.550 --> 00:15:41.370
Maybe we could have lunch together at Michelle's.

00:15:41.370 --> 00:15:42.370
Do you remember that?

00:15:42.370 --> 00:15:47.820
Michelle's. It's been years since we've been there.

00:15:47.820 --> 00:15:51.420
Please forgive me.

00:15:51.420 --> 00:15:53.420
I've been trying to explain to Mr. Trevor,

00:15:53.420 --> 00:15:56.370
but he insists upon speaking to you personally, Bishop.

00:15:56.370 --> 00:15:58.370
He could be fine.

00:16:04.120 --> 00:16:05.940
Matilda, will you keep the soup warm, please?

00:16:06.000 --> 00:16:07.140
The bishop's been called to the phone.

00:16:07.500 --> 00:16:09.660
Oh, and tell Delia that we'll be out for lunch tomorrow.

00:16:09.840 --> 00:16:10.460
Yes, ma'am.

00:16:12.290 --> 00:16:13.620
Of course, Mr. Trevor.

00:16:14.460 --> 00:16:17.570
Yes, but I appreciate your difficulties.

00:16:18.210 --> 00:16:21.760
Very well, I'll be there.

00:16:22.500 --> 00:16:24.720
Ten-thirty tomorrow morning, then we can go on to the board meeting.

00:16:25.390 --> 00:16:26.310
All right. Yes.

00:16:27.160 --> 00:16:27.440
Goodbye.

00:16:28.650 --> 00:16:30.000
Put that down, then, will you?

00:16:30.030 --> 00:16:33.670
Mr. Trevor's office, 10:30, tomorrow, and the board meeting in the bankers' club at 11:00.

00:16:33.720 --> 00:16:37.010
- Yes, sir. Tomorrow, Thursday. - You might as well go home now.

00:16:37.310 --> 00:16:40.450
- Well, there's a great deal of work to do, sir. - Never mind. You must be very tired.

00:16:40.570 --> 00:16:44.960
- Oh, thank you, sir. And don't forget, tomorrow you speak at the junior assembly at the Hotel Hamilton.

00:16:44.980 --> 00:16:47.060
- What time is that? - It's at luncheon, one o'clock.

00:16:47.180 --> 00:16:49.220
Remember, you made the appointment over a month ago.

00:16:49.240 --> 00:16:53.430
- Good night, Bishop. - Good night.

00:17:08.600 --> 00:17:13.830
Can't you help me? Can't you tell me? Please help me.

00:17:33.100 --> 00:18:00.000
yes good evening do you that isn't the question

00:18:01.340 --> 00:18:05.050
What can I do for you?

00:18:05.050 --> 00:18:09.350
Look, I'm afraid I must ask you to telephone my secretary of an appointment.

00:18:09.350 --> 00:18:10.350
I'm in the middle of dinner.

00:18:10.350 --> 00:18:11.350
I know, Henry.

00:18:11.350 --> 00:18:14.730
Your soup will keep warm.

00:18:14.730 --> 00:18:15.720
You asked for help.

00:18:15.720 --> 00:18:17.590
I?

00:18:17.590 --> 00:18:20.840
Who told you I asked for help?

00:18:20.840 --> 00:18:25.000
Well, you were known to be a good man, Henry, and you were heard.

00:18:25.000 --> 00:18:29.380
I was instructed to come here and answer to your prayer.

00:18:29.380 --> 00:18:30.000
Who are you?

00:18:30.280 --> 00:18:34.280
- I'm an angel. - I beg your pardon?

00:18:34.580 --> 00:18:38.120
I'm an angel. No wings at the moment, but...

00:18:38.200 --> 00:18:44.010
You're an angel. Oh, I knew it. I've been working too hard.

00:18:48.950 --> 00:18:52.160
I understand, Henry. It's hard to believe, even for you.

00:18:52.970 --> 00:18:55.180
Of course, I'm not one of the more important angels.

00:18:55.560 --> 00:18:58.260
I just happen to be assigned to this district temporarily.

00:18:58.940 --> 00:18:59.860
You see...

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:06.280
We're everywhere, helping people who deserve to be... to be held.

00:19:07.790 --> 00:19:11.790
As you're walking through the streets of the city, you may suddenly look into a strange face.

00:19:11.880 --> 00:19:14.880
It may be the face of a murderer, or it may be the face of an angel.

00:19:14.980 --> 00:19:21.110
You have some problems with the building of this cathedral, haven't you?

00:19:21.250 --> 00:19:23.900
- Yes. - A fine cathedral.

00:19:24.040 --> 00:19:28.420
It ought to look magnificent up there on the top of Sanctuary Hill.

00:19:32.460 --> 00:19:39.800
well henry do you believe i am what i say i am well how can i i've only got your word for it

00:19:39.800 --> 00:19:46.220
but you're a bishop you of all people can trust the word of an angel i'd like to what do you what

00:19:46.220 --> 00:19:52.850
do you propose to do perform a miracle if necessary well why don't you why don't you create the

00:19:52.850 --> 00:19:57.720
cathedral with one wave of your hand you wouldn't want me to do that would you how would you explain it

00:19:57.720 --> 00:20:00.000
well i tell the world you're being physically

00:20:00.030 --> 00:20:06.270
visited by an angel. You can't do that.

00:20:06.270 --> 00:20:08.090
Henry, is anything wrong?

00:20:08.090 --> 00:20:10.380
Oh, I'm sorry. I didn't know that you...

00:20:10.380 --> 00:20:12.430
Julia, if you don't mind.

00:20:12.430 --> 00:20:15.350
How do you do, Julia? I'm Dudley.

00:20:15.350 --> 00:20:19.630
Henry is engaging me to help him with his work.

00:20:19.630 --> 00:20:21.630
You mean you're going to be his assistant?

00:20:21.630 --> 00:20:23.630
That's it. I'm going to try to help Henry to rest

00:20:23.630 --> 00:20:25.780
and get some relaxation.

00:20:25.780 --> 00:20:27.670
That's what I've been praying for.

00:20:27.670 --> 00:20:28.720
You too?

00:20:28.720 --> 00:20:30.000
Oh, Henry, I'm so relieved.

00:20:30.570 --> 00:20:33.170
- Isn't it wonderful you've found someone to help you? - Yes, but...

00:20:33.270 --> 00:20:35.670
- Where do you come from, Dudley? - Oh, all around.

00:20:35.830 --> 00:20:40.400
- Yes, but where? - Julia, to tell you the truth, he says that he's in a...

00:20:41.560 --> 00:20:43.860
I've been doing some social service work downtown.

00:20:44.400 --> 00:20:46.600
Oh, and now you're going to be with Henry permanently?

00:20:46.700 --> 00:20:48.240
For as long as may be necessary.

00:20:48.930 --> 00:20:52.100
Julia, if you don't mind, I must talk to this gentleman alone.

00:20:52.200 --> 00:20:54.130
- I'll see you in a moment. - Sure.

00:20:54.310 --> 00:20:57.360
Oh, we were just having dinner. Won't you join us?

00:20:57.460 --> 00:21:00.000
That's very kind of you, but I have a number of things to do around town.

00:21:00.730 --> 00:21:05.430
- You can understand, Henry. So many people making so many mistakes. - Yes, I see you.

00:21:05.530 --> 00:21:08.840
- Then we'll see you tomorrow? - Oh, yes. Bright and early.

00:21:08.940 --> 00:21:13.980
Good. Whenever you're ready, Henry. Good night, Dudley.

00:21:14.740 --> 00:21:24.260
- Good night, Julia. - Are you sure you're an angel?

00:21:24.510 --> 00:21:28.130
I know it isn't easy for you, Henry, but you've just got to take me on faith.

00:21:28.250 --> 00:21:30.000
Yes, but for how long? How long will it take?

00:21:30.350 --> 00:21:33.990
for just long enough, until you can utter another prayer

00:21:34.140 --> 00:21:36.430
and say that you have no further need of me.

00:21:36.680 --> 00:21:39.560
Then I'll be gone and forgotten.

00:21:40.100 --> 00:21:42.840
But now, Julia is waiting for you at the dining table.

00:21:42.920 --> 00:21:50.000
- You must go to her. - Yes, but I don't...

00:22:32.810 --> 00:22:34.680
- Henry? - Yes?

00:22:35.150 --> 00:22:40.080
- What's the rest of Dudley's name? - I don't know.

00:23:10.080 --> 00:23:13.990
- The bishop didn't eat his breakfast. - No, he took only his prune juice.

00:23:14.260 --> 00:23:17.690
- Prune juice? What's the matter, does he say? - He looked awful.

00:23:17.790 --> 00:23:20.310
He said he had a very bad sleepless night.

00:23:21.330 --> 00:23:26.190
Passing up a breakfast like that, it just ain't normal.

00:23:26.290 --> 00:23:29.250
Nobody expects him to be normal. He's a bishop.

00:23:43.150 --> 00:23:45.590
- I'll get you something, sir. - No, thank you, Matilda.

00:23:45.670 --> 00:23:47.960
- Maybe just a cup of tea? - Nothing, thank you.

00:23:48.080 --> 00:23:52.300
- Good morning, Miss Castle. - Good morning, Bishop.

00:23:55.160 --> 00:23:57.210
Did anything come in for the cathedral fund?

00:23:57.560 --> 00:24:00.000
Mr. and Mrs. J. Thurston Ward. No contribution.

00:24:00.670 --> 00:24:02.370
of Gerald Wilmoth, $15.

00:24:02.490 --> 00:24:05.250
$15? We had a dime of $10,000.

00:24:05.730 --> 00:24:07.830
There's a letter from her explaining that this year...

00:24:07.930 --> 00:24:10.340
I know. Same letter they all write.

00:24:10.870 --> 00:24:12.970
I put your personal mail on the desk, Bishop.

00:24:13.090 --> 00:24:14.600
The rest I'm taking over to the office.

00:24:15.050 --> 00:24:16.550
I'll be there after the meetings.

00:24:42.830 --> 00:24:50.110
but I think there's someone at the door there.

00:24:50.110 --> 00:24:52.610
I'm Dundee, the bishop's new assistant.

00:24:52.610 --> 00:24:54.710
Good morning, Henry. Oh, it's a wonderful morning.

00:24:54.710 --> 00:24:57.030
I'm afraid I'm a little late, but I stopped to chat

00:24:57.030 --> 00:24:59.570
with a traffic policeman who was worried about his wife.

00:25:00.890 --> 00:25:01.890
Thank you. Thank you, dear.

00:25:02.490 --> 00:25:04.770
So I directed the traffic while he telephoned the hospital.

00:25:05.010 --> 00:25:05.350
I see.

00:25:05.510 --> 00:25:07.070
She's doing fine. So is the baby.

00:25:08.200 --> 00:25:11.310
Why, you must be Mildred Cassoway.

00:25:11.810 --> 00:25:12.390
How do you do?

00:25:12.680 --> 00:25:13.380
How do you do?

00:25:13.970 --> 00:25:15.770
We're going to be working together.

00:25:15.930 --> 00:25:16.750
Oh, that's very nice.

00:25:17.800 --> 00:25:20.700
Oh, thank you. Thank you very much.

00:25:20.900 --> 00:25:21.640
See you later, Mildred.

00:25:23.070 --> 00:25:25.780
Ready for duty.

00:25:26.260 --> 00:25:28.390
Completely at your service.

00:25:37.960 --> 00:25:47.210
- Good morning, Julia. - Lovely day.

00:25:47.310 --> 00:25:51.550
- Lovely. - And Henry and I are going out together.

00:25:51.650 --> 00:25:53.650
I'm terribly sorry, but I find I have some appointments.

00:25:53.750 --> 00:25:55.460
- You what? - It was Mr. Trevor.

00:25:55.560 --> 00:25:58.980
I got to see him at the board meeting, and after that, the junior assembly.

00:25:59.080 --> 00:26:00.000
Don't you remember Henry?

00:26:00.280 --> 00:26:02.430
- You promised me. - I know I did.

00:26:02.530 --> 00:26:06.140
Well, Dudley could represent you at those meetings, couldn't he?

00:26:06.240 --> 00:26:08.570
Could I?

00:26:08.670 --> 00:26:09.970
Oh, no, that's out of the question.

00:26:10.070 --> 00:26:16.250
You see, they expect me to would never do if I sent an assistant.

00:26:16.350 --> 00:26:28.250
Excuse me.

00:26:28.350 --> 00:26:30.000
You see, the trouble is, I can't explain.

00:26:30.030 --> 00:26:33.110
This is the way it is and this is the way it will always be.

00:26:33.110 --> 00:26:35.110
We've just got to get used to it, that's all.

00:26:35.110 --> 00:26:38.570
All right, I'll tell Matilda that she can have the day off for Christmas shopping

00:26:38.570 --> 00:26:40.570
and I'll take care of Debbie.

00:26:40.570 --> 00:26:57.160
Mrs. George B. Hamilton has pledged one million dollars

00:26:57.160 --> 00:26:59.170
but has not yet sent her cheque.

00:26:59.170 --> 00:27:00.000
Never mind those cash.

00:27:00.120 --> 00:27:02.480
That's work for a bookkeeper, not an angel.

00:27:02.560 --> 00:27:04.680
- Work for a bookkeeper. - Aha.

00:27:04.710 --> 00:27:07.370
So, you're beginning to believe in me, huh?

00:27:07.450 --> 00:27:10.330
I don't know who you are. I don't know where you came from or who it was that sent you.

00:27:10.410 --> 00:27:13.410
Whatever it was, I just wish you'd make haste. There's no time to lose.

00:27:13.430 --> 00:27:17.750
- Because the cathedral must be built? - Well, obviously, that's the most important thing.

00:27:17.830 --> 00:27:19.840
Oh, because Julia must be happy.

00:27:19.910 --> 00:27:22.570
You know, Henry, it's going to be difficult for me to help you

00:27:22.650 --> 00:27:24.650
until I'm sure what it is you really want.

00:27:24.730 --> 00:27:28.420
Well, I've got the... then the... oh.

00:27:44.430 --> 00:27:50.880
- Would you mind telling me what you intend to do now? - This card index file is an awful mess. I think I'll reorganize it.

00:27:51.160 --> 00:27:57.720
- I still think you're wasting your time on unimportant details. - Ah, nothing's unimportant. Remember, we are interested even in the lowliest sparrow.

00:28:49.360 --> 00:28:51.860
- Hello, Debbie. - Are you Dudley?

00:28:52.050 --> 00:28:54.000
Yes. How did you know?

00:28:54.150 --> 00:28:57.590
Mommy told me. She said you came to help Daddy.

00:28:57.890 --> 00:28:58.930
That's right.

00:29:00.540 --> 00:29:04.490
- You said you were very nice. - That's extremely kind of mummy.

00:29:04.490 --> 00:29:09.310
Mummy said that maybe with you here, we walk out to see Daddy sometimes.

00:29:09.310 --> 00:29:12.160
- Maybe we will. - That'll be enough out of you, Debbie.

00:29:12.160 --> 00:29:15.840
I asked Matilda to put your lunch on a tray for you.

00:29:15.840 --> 00:29:17.800
Thank you, Julia. I'll get along very well.

00:29:17.800 --> 00:29:20.060
I'm sure you will. Come on, dear.

00:29:20.060 --> 00:29:22.890
- Goodbye, Dudley. - Goodbye, Debbie.

00:29:22.890 --> 00:29:24.890
- Goodbye. - Bye.

00:30:11.480 --> 00:30:13.160
Oh, thank you, Matilda.

00:30:13.540 --> 00:30:14.860
Aren't you going to wear a hat?

00:30:15.150 --> 00:30:15.930
I never use one.

00:30:16.330 --> 00:30:17.990
Oh, it's very cold out.

00:30:18.110 --> 00:30:19.610
Oh, well, the cold never affects me.

00:30:19.930 --> 00:30:22.010
I think you should wear this anyway.

00:30:22.440 --> 00:30:25.220
I bought it for the bishop last Christmas.

00:30:26.650 --> 00:30:28.820
Oh, it's a lovely scarf, Matilda.

00:30:29.160 --> 00:30:30.000
I'm sure the bishop will...

00:30:30.000 --> 00:30:31.920
He'll appreciate it when he sees them on me.

00:30:32.000 --> 00:30:34.550
- Well, thank you. Goodbye. - Bye.

00:31:01.240 --> 00:31:09.060
too little or something oh no no why that's the most ridiculous thing i've ever heard

00:31:11.170 --> 00:31:28.840
come on debbie we'll show them how wrong they are hey fellas hey fellas who's the head man around here

00:31:31.340 --> 00:31:36.320
what's the idea of this game you're playing this isn't a game this is a battle we attack the port

00:31:36.320 --> 00:31:42.160
and they try to defend us see oh yeah now i see now this young lady would like to get into it who

00:31:42.160 --> 00:31:47.490
her she can't fight her father's a bishop but what difference does it make what her father is

00:31:47.490 --> 00:31:52.410
and you high hat how would you like her to play on your team she couldn't throw a snowball as far

00:31:52.410 --> 00:32:00.000
as i could spit couldn't shake come on debbie you show them

00:32:00.000 --> 00:32:00.640
what you can do.

00:32:00.920 --> 00:32:01.740
But it's true.

00:32:02.020 --> 00:32:03.160
I can't throw him.

00:32:03.200 --> 00:32:03.560
Nonsense.

00:32:03.720 --> 00:32:04.420
Of course you can.

00:32:04.500 --> 00:32:04.840
Now, here.

00:32:05.270 --> 00:32:05.950
Back it tight.

00:32:06.090 --> 00:32:06.970
Put it in that hand.

00:32:07.310 --> 00:32:08.190
Draw the arm back.

00:32:08.600 --> 00:32:09.380
Aim it right at him.

00:32:09.540 --> 00:32:10.180
And let it go.

00:32:13.080 --> 00:32:14.440
Beautiful.

00:32:14.940 --> 00:32:15.720
A bullseye.

00:32:16.080 --> 00:32:17.460
She pitched a curve.

00:32:18.030 --> 00:32:19.170
Did you see it, Bob?

00:32:20.590 --> 00:32:21.550
Come on, kid.

00:32:21.890 --> 00:32:22.910
You're in our army.

00:32:22.910 --> 00:32:24.230
Keep looking at them around.

00:32:24.630 --> 00:32:25.170
Come on.

00:32:25.290 --> 00:32:25.650
Come on.

00:32:29.250 --> 00:32:29.670
Come on.

00:32:32.720 --> 00:32:35.740
- Don't you get hurt? - Probably, but she'll love it. May I?

00:32:35.740 --> 00:32:43.350
- Surely. - Dudley, what are you doing out here?

00:32:43.350 --> 00:32:47.820
- Just admiring the scenery. - But aren't you supposed to be working?

00:32:47.820 --> 00:32:51.980
I always take a walk before lunch. Good idea of relaxing, you know.

00:32:51.980 --> 00:32:57.090
- Oh, I wish you could persuade Henry to do that. - I'll try.

00:32:57.090 --> 00:33:00.000
Oh, by the way, I told Miss Cassoway to go home, and I told Dee

00:33:00.000 --> 00:33:02.310
not to bother to bring me anything on the tray.

00:33:02.310 --> 00:33:04.050
Well, what would you do about lunch?

00:33:04.050 --> 00:33:06.560
I thought I'd go to Michelle's.

00:33:06.560 --> 00:33:07.750
Ever heard of it?

00:33:07.750 --> 00:33:09.030
Michelle's?

00:33:09.030 --> 00:33:11.490
Oh, it's a lovely place.

00:33:11.490 --> 00:33:12.690
We used to go there.

00:33:12.690 --> 00:33:13.860
That was years ago.

00:33:13.860 --> 00:33:16.870
Well, well, how about you and I going there today?

00:33:16.870 --> 00:33:19.310
You and... to Michelle?

00:33:19.310 --> 00:33:20.910
Oh, no, no, I couldn't.

00:33:20.910 --> 00:33:21.810
Why not?

00:33:21.810 --> 00:33:23.970
Surely you don't think any would mind.

00:33:23.970 --> 00:33:25.380
Oh, I'd explain to him if he just...

00:33:25.380 --> 00:33:27.490
Oh, no, no, no, it isn't that, but...

00:33:27.490 --> 00:33:29.170
Well, you see, Matilda's all Christmas shopping,

00:33:29.170 --> 00:33:30.000
and so I have to look at it.

00:33:30.000 --> 00:33:34.870
Well, here's Matilda now.

00:33:34.870 --> 00:33:40.270
If you wish...

00:33:40.270 --> 00:33:41.270
Oh, hello.

00:33:41.270 --> 00:33:42.270
Hello.

00:33:42.270 --> 00:33:44.270
If you wish, I'll take Debbie home, Mrs. Brom.

00:33:44.270 --> 00:33:45.270
But, Matilda, you're shopping it.

00:33:45.270 --> 00:33:46.270
Oh, I finished it.

00:33:46.270 --> 00:33:49.270
I finished it so quick, it was just like a miracle.

00:33:49.270 --> 00:33:51.950
We won!

00:33:51.950 --> 00:33:54.350
Debbie, that's wonderful!

00:33:54.350 --> 00:33:56.350
Congratulations!

00:33:56.350 --> 00:33:57.350
Come on!

00:33:57.350 --> 00:33:58.710
We're getting out of here!

00:34:15.550 --> 00:34:17.050
- Welcome. - Oh, Michelle.

00:34:17.150 --> 00:34:19.750
- It's been a long time. - Oh, much too long.

00:34:19.850 --> 00:34:22.050
- But you know my husband's work. - Yes, your husband.

00:34:22.150 --> 00:34:24.300
But he doesn't come to see us anymore.

00:34:24.400 --> 00:34:28.450
But we understand. We understand. This way.

00:34:34.700 --> 00:34:37.890
- It is dissatisfactory, Monsieur. - Fine, thank you.

00:34:38.140 --> 00:34:40.280
- Parfum. - Friends of yours?

00:34:40.440 --> 00:34:44.010
Yes. They are members of the cathedral committee.

00:34:44.130 --> 00:34:45.630
- Madame? - Thank you.

00:34:45.800 --> 00:34:47.320
- Monsieur? - No, thank you, Michelle.

00:34:47.440 --> 00:34:49.520
Julia, don't bother to look all through then.

00:34:49.640 --> 00:34:52.600
Michelle, tell you what you do. You just bring us the best lunch that you can think of.

00:34:52.720 --> 00:34:54.440
Oh, I can see Monsieur is a gourmet.

00:34:54.560 --> 00:34:57.960
Perhaps Monsieur and Madame would be interested in a Guy de Haen à la Michelle.

00:34:58.080 --> 00:34:59.960
Et j'ai des trucs français. Excuse me.

00:35:00.280 --> 00:35:01.760
"Je préfère les Italiennes."

00:35:01.760 --> 00:35:03.900
"Etre vitalienne, quel sacrilège."

00:35:03.980 --> 00:35:05.740
"Et par tous les anges du ciel, monsieur, laissez-moi faire."

00:35:05.820 --> 00:35:07.820
Oh, no, please, monsieur, let's leave heaven out of this.

00:35:07.900 --> 00:35:09.240
"Je préfère les Italiennes."

00:35:09.320 --> 00:35:11.740
"Et surtout, pas de cayenne, du paprika."

00:35:11.820 --> 00:35:14.990
Du paprika? Très bien. Du paprika. Du paprika.

00:35:15.090 --> 00:35:16.830
You speak French beautifully.

00:35:16.870 --> 00:35:19.370
Uh, I've had quite a bit of work to do in Paris.

00:35:19.450 --> 00:35:24.640
Oh. Dudley, I... I've been wondering about you.

00:35:24.680 --> 00:35:26.370
Wondering about me? Why?

00:35:26.450 --> 00:35:29.730
You know so much. It makes me feel uncomfortable.

00:35:30.380 --> 00:35:36.010
- In that case, I'm sorry I learned anything. - But I'm glad you knew about Michelle.

00:35:37.230 --> 00:35:44.530
- It's so nice to be back here again. So nice. - Do you have memories of this place, Julia?

00:35:44.740 --> 00:35:47.510
- Mm-hmm. - Did you and Henry come here often?

00:35:47.930 --> 00:35:51.960
Yes. As a matter of fact, this is where we became engaged to be married.

00:35:52.180 --> 00:35:55.890
Ah. Then I can understand why you love it.

00:35:55.890 --> 00:36:00.000
- Oh, hello. - Pardon me.

00:36:00.510 --> 00:36:02.550
Oh, thank you.

00:36:02.550 --> 00:36:03.550
How about that?

00:36:03.550 --> 00:36:05.170
My.

00:36:05.170 --> 00:36:07.410
Would you care to have your palm read?

00:36:07.410 --> 00:36:08.410
Oh, no.

00:36:08.410 --> 00:36:09.350
No, thank you.

00:36:09.350 --> 00:36:10.900
Would you?

00:36:10.900 --> 00:36:11.900
Oh, no, thanks.

00:36:11.900 --> 00:36:13.920
I know too much about myself as it is.

00:36:13.920 --> 00:36:14.820
Oh, you are different.

00:36:14.820 --> 00:36:16.840
I know so little about myself.

00:36:16.840 --> 00:36:19.050
May I look at your hand?

00:36:19.050 --> 00:36:21.160
Oh, can you do that too?

00:36:21.160 --> 00:36:22.160
It's not too difficult.

00:36:22.160 --> 00:36:24.160
I suppose you can read the future.

00:36:24.160 --> 00:36:26.360
Within limits.

00:36:26.360 --> 00:36:30.000
He's holding her hand.

00:36:30.000 --> 00:36:33.680
I never noticed.

00:36:33.680 --> 00:36:34.680
Your eyes are green.

00:36:34.680 --> 00:36:38.870
I see a great deal of happiness.

00:36:38.870 --> 00:36:41.490
I see a woman who's adored.

00:36:41.490 --> 00:36:44.180
I see a rich, full life.

00:36:44.180 --> 00:36:47.630
Do you see Henry's new cathedral?

00:36:47.630 --> 00:36:48.630
No, that's not very clear.

00:36:48.630 --> 00:36:50.630
There's kind of a fuzziness about that.

00:36:50.630 --> 00:36:51.710
And Debbie?

00:36:51.710 --> 00:36:52.820
No need to worry about her.

00:36:52.820 --> 00:36:55.270
Just thinking.

00:36:55.270 --> 00:36:58.510
The world changes, but two things remain constant.

00:37:00.670 --> 00:37:03.930
Now they're really one and the same thing.

00:37:03.930 --> 00:37:08.540
The trouble is people grow old.

00:37:08.540 --> 00:37:09.710
Not everybody.

00:37:09.710 --> 00:37:12.820
The only people who grow old were born old to begin with.

00:37:12.820 --> 00:37:14.220
You were born young.

00:37:14.220 --> 00:37:16.270
You'll remain that way.

00:37:16.270 --> 00:37:18.950
I wish I could find you.

00:37:18.950 --> 00:37:20.260
You may.

00:37:20.260 --> 00:37:25.250
You haven't looked at my hand once.

00:37:25.250 --> 00:37:28.170
I never know what to think of you, Dudley.

00:37:28.170 --> 00:37:30.000
I never know whether you're joking or not.

00:37:30.000 --> 00:37:33.280
I'm at my most serious when I'm joking.

00:37:33.360 --> 00:37:41.420
Excuse me, Juliet. Gotta do something about that.

00:37:41.500 --> 00:37:46.190
Do forgive me coming to your table, ladies.

00:37:46.270 --> 00:37:48.690
My name is Dudley. I believe you're all good friends of Juliet's.

00:37:48.770 --> 00:37:51.030
And we've been wondering if you'd care to join us for lunch.

00:37:51.110 --> 00:37:54.120
- Oh, no, I thank you. - Why don't you touch for some coffee?

00:37:54.200 --> 00:37:56.540
- How 'bout that? Do come over. - Don't think we should be...

00:37:56.620 --> 00:38:00.000
- It's only more delighted. - What a beautiful lady.

00:38:00.030 --> 00:38:01.030
Oh, thank you, sir.

00:38:01.030 --> 00:38:02.030
Very nice.

00:38:02.030 --> 00:38:03.030
Thank goodness.

00:38:03.030 --> 00:38:04.030
Yes.

00:38:04.030 --> 00:38:05.030
She's right over here.

00:38:05.030 --> 00:38:06.030
Yes, sir.

00:38:06.030 --> 00:38:07.030
Hello, Mrs. Castor.

00:38:07.030 --> 00:38:08.030
How are you?

00:38:08.030 --> 00:38:09.030
Good to see you.

00:38:09.030 --> 00:38:09.920
Good to see you.

00:38:09.920 --> 00:38:10.920
Good to see you.

00:38:10.920 --> 00:38:11.920
Good to see you.

00:38:11.920 --> 00:38:12.920
Good to see you.

00:38:12.920 --> 00:38:13.920
Good to see you.

00:38:13.920 --> 00:38:14.920
Michelle, bring us three Benedictines.

00:38:14.920 --> 00:38:15.920
Three Benedictines, yes, sir.

00:38:15.920 --> 00:38:16.920
No, no, no.

00:38:16.920 --> 00:38:17.920
Make it three stingers.

00:38:17.920 --> 00:38:18.740
Stingers?

00:38:18.740 --> 00:38:19.740
Wait a minute, sir.

00:38:19.740 --> 00:38:25.260
Julia?

00:38:25.260 --> 00:38:30.000
Julia?

00:38:31.660 --> 00:38:34.620
- Julia. - Oh.

00:38:34.700 --> 00:38:38.000
- Mattel, is lunch ready? - Why, sir, we thought you were out for lunch.

00:38:38.080 --> 00:38:40.660
I cancelled my appointment. Are Mrs. Braum and Debbie home?

00:38:40.740 --> 00:38:43.120
- Debbie's here, sir. - Mrs. Braum, where's she?

00:38:43.320 --> 00:38:46.420
Why, sir, she went out to lunch with Mr. Dudley.

00:38:47.710 --> 00:38:50.770
- With Dudley? - Why, yes, sir.

00:38:51.640 --> 00:38:56.280
- I thought you knew, sir. - Yes, of course.

00:39:15.040 --> 00:39:19.550
you know santa claus doesn't really look like that

00:39:19.550 --> 00:39:21.570
oh you know santa claus

00:39:21.570 --> 00:39:23.210
certainly not if he is nice chap

00:39:23.210 --> 00:39:25.160
well in that case you must tell debbie about it

00:39:25.160 --> 00:39:27.980
he's just beginning to be a little bit doubtful

00:39:27.980 --> 00:39:28.840
ha ha ha

00:39:34.000 --> 00:39:35.000
You like that hat?

00:39:35.000 --> 00:39:36.000
Yes, I'm crazy about it.

00:39:36.000 --> 00:39:39.810
Well, let's go ahead and buy it.

00:39:39.810 --> 00:39:40.810
Oh, no.

00:39:40.810 --> 00:39:41.720
I couldn't.

00:39:41.720 --> 00:39:42.820
It's much too, uh...

00:39:42.820 --> 00:39:43.820
Too what?

00:39:43.820 --> 00:39:44.820
Too attractive?

00:39:44.820 --> 00:39:45.680
Nothing could be...

00:39:45.680 --> 00:39:47.680
Why, my old friend, the professor.

00:39:47.680 --> 00:39:48.680
Oh, professor.

00:39:48.680 --> 00:39:49.950
Julia!

00:39:49.950 --> 00:39:50.950
Are you with this man?

00:39:50.950 --> 00:39:51.950
Yes, of course.

00:39:51.950 --> 00:39:53.060
It's Dudley.

00:39:53.060 --> 00:39:54.060
The professor knows me well.

00:39:54.060 --> 00:39:55.060
The University of Vienna.

00:39:55.060 --> 00:39:57.060
Yes, I've been thinking about that.

00:39:57.060 --> 00:40:00.000
I don't believe you've ever been in Vienna in your life.

00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:01.800
He always pretends he's never seen me before.

00:40:01.900 --> 00:40:04.500
I don't know who he is, Junior, but I don't trust him.

00:40:04.600 --> 00:40:06.500
Professor, he's Henry's new assistant.

00:40:06.600 --> 00:40:09.550
Oh, you mean you know this fellow?

00:40:09.650 --> 00:40:10.550
Of course I do.

00:40:10.650 --> 00:40:14.100
Well, in that case, how about dropping into my humble diggings

00:40:14.200 --> 00:40:15.600
for a bit of Yuletide cheer?

00:40:15.700 --> 00:40:17.700
No, no, I have to go home.

00:40:17.800 --> 00:40:21.320
Well, perhaps just for a few minutes.

00:40:21.420 --> 00:40:22.720
Good, come along.

00:40:22.820 --> 00:40:30.000
There's a little shepherd.

00:40:30.030 --> 00:40:33.050
It's rather inferior grade, but potable.

00:40:33.150 --> 00:40:36.510
Professor, I see you're quite a religious man.

00:40:36.610 --> 00:40:37.510
What makes you think that?

00:40:37.610 --> 00:40:39.510
You have an angel on your tree.

00:40:39.610 --> 00:40:41.500
Julia gave me that years ago.

00:40:41.600 --> 00:40:45.390
- Why, your tree is beautiful, Professor. - It's disgraceful.

00:40:45.490 --> 00:40:47.280
Oh, and it gives me the illusion of peace on earth,

00:40:47.380 --> 00:40:50.710
goodwill toward men to a charming lady.

00:40:50.810 --> 00:40:51.540
- Thank you. - Lovely.

00:40:51.640 --> 00:40:54.790
- You've noticed? - Isn't it more remarkable that you have?

00:40:54.890 --> 00:40:55.790
Remarkable.

00:40:55.890 --> 00:40:58.870
When you want to know about a woman, ask the old men.

00:40:58.970 --> 00:41:00.000
They know.

00:41:04.090 --> 00:41:06.850
Why don't you show us the manuscript of your book, Professor, will you?

00:41:07.470 --> 00:41:08.130
My book?

00:41:08.270 --> 00:41:08.910
Yes, please.

00:41:09.330 --> 00:41:10.280
Oh, no, no, no.

00:41:10.610 --> 00:41:11.610
You're writing one?

00:41:12.140 --> 00:41:12.620
Yes.

00:41:16.520 --> 00:41:17.500
You didn't know.

00:41:18.580 --> 00:41:19.680
You didn't tell me.

00:41:20.280 --> 00:41:24.760
I described that book in detail in the course of lectures I gave at the University of Vienna.

00:41:25.440 --> 00:41:26.800
All my pupils had.

00:41:27.400 --> 00:41:30.000
Now I'm sitting, this fellow's an imposter.

00:41:30.510 --> 00:41:33.230
Oh, that book. I thought you'd finished that years ago.

00:41:33.430 --> 00:41:36.690
I'll tell you... I'll tell you about my book.

00:41:37.630 --> 00:41:39.600
For 20 years, I've been talking about it.

00:41:39.850 --> 00:41:42.650
I've been promising the publishers that it would be delivered next spring.

00:41:43.130 --> 00:41:46.620
The funny part of it is that in all that time, I haven't written one word.

00:41:47.130 --> 00:41:48.210
Not one word.

00:41:48.390 --> 00:41:49.110
Why not?

00:41:49.310 --> 00:41:51.310
Well, I couldn't think of anything original to say.

00:41:51.510 --> 00:42:00.000
It's the same old monotonous history, dry as dust, the whole story of my life.

00:42:00.030 --> 00:42:06.040
Frustration. It's a chronic disease, and it's incurable.

00:42:06.040 --> 00:42:09.010
Once I was madly...

00:42:09.010 --> 00:42:19.960
Once I was madly in love with a girl.

00:42:19.960 --> 00:42:23.290
My friends, she was a vision of delight.

00:42:23.290 --> 00:42:25.720
A pure enchantress.

00:42:25.720 --> 00:42:26.720
You never told me about that.

00:42:26.720 --> 00:42:28.720
No, that's just the trouble.

00:42:28.720 --> 00:42:30.000
I never told her.

00:42:30.120 --> 00:42:33.880
I didn't care about it either, couldn't find the words.

00:42:33.960 --> 00:42:36.420
So, she married an athlete,

00:42:36.920 --> 00:42:41.500
a great hulking wolf who never even reached the 8th grade.

00:42:42.510 --> 00:42:45.840
But he knew how to say, "I love you."

00:42:48.180 --> 00:42:52.880
Same trouble with my book, can't find the words.

00:42:53.450 --> 00:42:55.920
Even when you had this coin to inspire you?

00:42:57.570 --> 00:43:00.000
Well, that's the one that you gave to Henry,

00:43:00.120 --> 00:43:02.980
Yes, I stole it off the table.

00:43:02.980 --> 00:43:04.980
You wasted your time, Dudley. It's worthless.

00:43:04.980 --> 00:43:06.980
Oh, on the contrary.

00:43:06.980 --> 00:43:09.810
This is one of the rarest of all antiquities.

00:43:09.810 --> 00:43:13.860
Only 100 of these coins were minted by Julius Caesar 2,000 years ago.

00:43:13.860 --> 00:43:16.070
That was when Cleopatra visited Rome.

00:43:16.070 --> 00:43:19.240
Presumably, these coins were used to pay her hotel bill.

00:43:19.240 --> 00:43:21.200
I never knew.

00:43:21.200 --> 00:43:24.300
Nobody knew about it except Caesar's wife.

00:43:24.300 --> 00:43:26.460
She was suspicious.

00:43:26.460 --> 00:43:30.000
Definitely. She did not share her husband's admiration.

00:43:30.000 --> 00:43:36.200
for cleopatra so she had these coins destroyed melted into ornaments for herself this is the

00:43:36.200 --> 00:43:43.580
one she missed it's an unwritten chapter in history and you professor will write it do

00:43:43.580 --> 00:43:50.550
you know any more stories like that oh any number of them curious fellow dudley have you just begun

00:43:50.550 --> 00:44:00.000
to notice that what's your background my uh background well where do you come from and don't

00:44:00.000 --> 00:44:02.760
Don't tell me any more about Vienna, because I won't believe it.

00:44:04.140 --> 00:44:07.080
Supposing I told you I came from another planet, would you believe me?

00:44:07.870 --> 00:44:09.250
I don't know.

00:44:09.620 --> 00:44:10.880
I'd believe you, Dad.

00:44:11.060 --> 00:44:13.030
And you'd be right, Julia, as always.

00:44:13.350 --> 00:44:15.140
We all come from our own little planets.

00:44:15.530 --> 00:44:16.680
That's why we're all different.

00:44:16.810 --> 00:44:18.240
That's what makes life interesting.

00:44:34.320 --> 00:44:36.460
We don't seem to be making any headway.

00:44:38.770 --> 00:44:40.850
First star I see tonight.

00:44:41.290 --> 00:44:44.470
You must make a wish, Julia.

00:44:45.270 --> 00:44:48.310
Oh, it's getting dark.

00:44:48.480 --> 00:44:49.680
It must be late.

00:44:49.960 --> 00:44:52.000
Henry will be worried. We must be leaving.

00:44:52.100 --> 00:44:53.100
Oh, no.

00:44:53.200 --> 00:44:55.200
Yes, I'm sorry, Professor, but we must.

00:44:55.220 --> 00:44:58.380
Dudley.

00:44:58.520 --> 00:45:00.000
Yes, my friend. There's one thing.

00:45:00.000 --> 00:45:02.820
One thing that troubles me. One thing I wish I knew.

00:45:02.820 --> 00:45:03.960
What's that?

00:45:03.960 --> 00:45:07.440
Well, I'm an old man, and that history is a tremendous task.

00:45:07.440 --> 00:45:11.840
I wonder, will I have time to finish it?

00:45:11.840 --> 00:45:16.080
You'll finish your history, Professor. You'll have time.

00:45:16.080 --> 00:45:20.070
I believe you, Dad, honey.

00:45:20.070 --> 00:45:23.990
You know, for quite a while now, every time I passed a cemetery,

00:45:23.990 --> 00:45:26.020
I felt as if I were apartment hunting.

00:45:30.000 --> 00:45:34.780
- Good old man, a very happy afternoon. - Thank you.

00:45:35.610 --> 00:45:37.560
- God bless you. - Thank you.

00:45:37.840 --> 00:45:39.600
I'll pass that recommendation along.

00:46:33.130 --> 00:46:34.800
Oh, my, that's pretty.

00:46:34.940 --> 00:46:38.210
Why don't you take some of that pink stuff and make little curlicues with it?

00:46:38.310 --> 00:46:40.110
Very good.

00:46:41.310 --> 00:46:43.410
- The dinner won't be spoiled, Matilda? - Oh, no, sir.

00:46:43.510 --> 00:46:46.700
I had a sort of feeling that they might be late.

00:46:47.290 --> 00:46:48.560
Very considerate of you.

00:46:48.960 --> 00:46:51.180
- What's that cake for? - What cake?

00:46:51.280 --> 00:46:54.740
- Cake behind you. - That cake?

00:46:54.800 --> 00:46:57.130
Oh, for anybody who might like cake, sir.

00:46:57.230 --> 00:47:00.000
But you know very well that neither Mrs. Brawl nor myself like these elaborate things.

00:47:00.090 --> 00:47:03.090
Oh, but we've baked you an egg custard, sir.

00:47:03.090 --> 00:47:14.440
I'm sorry we were late for dinner.

00:47:14.440 --> 00:47:15.440
Good evening.

00:47:15.440 --> 00:47:17.440
We've had the most marvelous time.

00:47:17.440 --> 00:47:19.680
Oh, I wish you'd been with us.

00:47:19.680 --> 00:47:20.680
Yes, I wish I had.

00:47:20.680 --> 00:47:21.680
Debbie told me all about the snow fight.

00:47:21.680 --> 00:47:22.680
Oh, did she?

00:47:22.680 --> 00:47:24.600
We went to see Professor Wutherich,

00:47:24.600 --> 00:47:26.600
and then we had lunch at Michelle's.

00:47:26.600 --> 00:47:27.590
Oh, is Debbie in bed yet?

00:47:27.590 --> 00:47:28.440
No, she's waiting to see you.

00:47:28.440 --> 00:47:29.450
Oh, good.

00:47:30.120 --> 00:47:32.190
Well, I'll just go up and sit down for her.

00:47:32.190 --> 00:47:36.330
I won't be a minute. I just want to see if she's all right.

00:47:36.330 --> 00:47:41.360
Do you trust you spent a profitable afternoon, Dudley?

00:47:41.360 --> 00:47:47.540
Oh, yes. Did you have a profitable afternoon, Henry?

00:47:47.540 --> 00:47:52.880
Not very. I'd like to see you for a moment.

00:47:52.880 --> 00:47:53.840
Certainly.

00:48:11.070 --> 00:48:13.050
Can you prove to me that you are an angel?

00:48:13.050 --> 00:48:15.240
Proof? You mean a document?

00:48:15.240 --> 00:48:19.270
Surely you, of all people, should know that an angel needs no passport.

00:48:19.270 --> 00:48:22.520
I want to see you perform a miracle.

00:48:22.520 --> 00:48:23.520
What kind?

00:48:23.520 --> 00:48:26.780
Well, make this desk rise up and fly around the room.

00:48:26.780 --> 00:48:30.000
Please, Henry, I didn't come down here to do silly tricks. I'm surprised.

00:48:30.000 --> 00:48:32.280
I don't believe you're an angel.

00:48:32.280 --> 00:48:35.110
I think you're a demon right out of--

00:48:35.110 --> 00:48:37.660
Oh, Henry. Don't say that.

00:48:37.660 --> 00:48:41.100
Well, anyway, you know how I feel.

00:48:41.100 --> 00:48:46.450
Now, wait a minute, Dudley. There's another thing.

00:49:25.120 --> 00:49:26.400
Come on.

00:49:30.250 --> 00:50:00.000
- Thank you, Dudley. - What we are about to receive, may the Lord make us truly thankful.

00:50:15.870 --> 00:50:19.910
Pass the salary, Henry. Please. The salary.

00:50:30.190 --> 00:50:59.700
Is that your humming?

00:51:00.030 --> 00:51:04.580
- Oh, I don't know, dear. Is it anything? - That's rather gay.

00:51:04.580 --> 00:51:14.230
- Oh, I feel gay. - I like to watch you brushing your hair.

00:51:14.230 --> 00:51:21.200
- Thank you, dear. Is that a compliment? - Yes, you do it so... so capably.

00:51:21.200 --> 00:51:29.540
- Thank you. - In fact...

00:51:30.120 --> 00:51:32.620
Now I come to think of it, everything you do is capable.

00:51:34.090 --> 00:51:39.230
If there's one thing I pride myself on, it's the fact that we lead a well-ordered life.

00:51:39.430 --> 00:51:40.720
The family, I mean.

00:51:41.080 --> 00:51:43.990
Of course, the credit for that is due to you much more than to me.

00:51:45.880 --> 00:51:48.180
- I think you're an excellent wife. - Well, thank you.

00:51:49.370 --> 00:51:52.120
- Do you think I'm an excellent husband? - Of course, dear.

00:51:52.990 --> 00:51:55.990
We're going to have an early supper so we can get to St. Timothy's on time.

00:51:56.190 --> 00:51:57.190
- St. Timothy's? - Mm-hmm.

00:51:57.390 --> 00:51:59.730
- For the choir rehearsal for the benefit. - Oh, yes.

00:52:00.190 --> 00:52:03.040
You know, you've been looking awfully tired lately.

00:52:03.370 --> 00:52:06.260
I hope you're going to take it easier now that Dudley's here with you.

00:52:06.910 --> 00:52:09.250
I think that he's very able.

00:52:09.370 --> 00:52:11.240
- You do? - Yes.

00:52:11.900 --> 00:52:15.200
- He knows so many things. - What, for instance?

00:52:15.840 --> 00:52:18.700
Well, you should have seen him with Professor Wurthridge.

00:52:19.010 --> 00:52:21.250
He knows more about history than the professor.

00:52:21.530 --> 00:52:22.600
He's been at it longer.

00:52:43.340 --> 00:52:46.130
- I know hundreds of stories. - Tell me one, please.

00:52:46.140 --> 00:52:47.990
All right, now, let me think.

00:52:48.340 --> 00:52:50.960
All right, now, this happened many, many years ago.

00:52:51.050 --> 00:52:53.040
That's not the way to begin.

00:52:53.210 --> 00:52:55.120
Stories start once upon a time.

00:52:55.200 --> 00:52:56.560
Oh, yes, that's true.

00:52:56.660 --> 00:53:00.000
Well, once upon a time, there was a little boy, and he lived.

00:53:02.680 --> 00:53:06.550
He was a shepherd, and the town where he lived was called Bethlehem.

00:53:06.550 --> 00:53:09.330
That's where the star was.

00:53:09.330 --> 00:53:12.330
That's right. Only David lived long before the star.

00:53:12.330 --> 00:53:15.380
Well, one night, David was out in the hills, tending his sheep.

00:53:15.380 --> 00:53:17.460
He was playing the harp and singing.

00:53:17.460 --> 00:53:19.440
What was he singing? Jingle bells?

00:53:19.440 --> 00:53:23.600
No, no. Jingle bells hadn't been written then.

00:53:23.600 --> 00:53:25.680
David was singing songs that he wrote himself.

00:53:25.680 --> 00:53:29.830
Well, suddenly, an angel came down and spoke to David.

00:53:30.480 --> 00:53:32.610
- How did David know it was an angel?

00:53:32.610 --> 00:53:33.690
- He didn't know.

00:53:33.690 --> 00:53:35.590
And you know, that's the way it always is.

00:53:35.590 --> 00:53:38.370
Angels come down and put ideas into people's heads,

00:53:38.370 --> 00:53:40.330
and then people feel very proud of themselves

00:53:40.330 --> 00:53:42.700
because they think it was all their own idea.

00:53:42.700 --> 00:53:45.150
Well, this angel said to David,

00:53:45.150 --> 00:53:47.450
one of your lambs has strayed.

00:53:47.450 --> 00:53:49.420
So David put aside his harp

00:53:49.420 --> 00:53:51.750
and went out in the darkness to find the lamb.

00:53:51.750 --> 00:53:53.860
Of course, the angel guided him.

00:53:53.860 --> 00:53:55.580
And when David found the lamb,

00:53:55.580 --> 00:53:59.030
he saw a great, big, ferocious lion.

00:53:59.030 --> 00:53:59.700
- Oh!

00:54:00.480 --> 00:54:06.920
so david said to the lion you get away from that lamb and the lion said you get away from me or i'll

00:54:06.920 --> 00:54:13.410
eat you too did david run away you know why because the angel put another idea into his head

00:54:13.410 --> 00:54:19.230
so david took out his sling and he hurled a stone and hit the lion right between the eyes

00:54:19.230 --> 00:54:26.020
i bet that lion was surprised and so was david because he didn't know an angel had helped him

00:54:26.020 --> 00:54:30.000
well he picked up the lamb and took him back to the fold and then he fell

00:54:30.030 --> 00:54:32.620
so happy that he made up another song.

00:54:32.630 --> 00:54:33.910
It started out:

00:54:34.040 --> 00:54:37.940
"The Lord is my shepherd, I shall not want."

00:54:38.000 --> 00:54:40.880
"He maketh me to lie down in green pastures."

00:54:40.880 --> 00:54:43.840
"He leadeth me beside the still waters."

00:54:43.860 --> 00:54:46.800
"He restoreth my soul."

00:54:46.840 --> 00:54:50.550
You can tell the rest of this, Henry.

00:54:50.720 --> 00:54:53.110
Some other time.

00:54:53.200 --> 00:54:54.330
Hello, Daddy.

00:54:54.390 --> 00:54:59.030
Miss Caswell, will you get Mrs. Hamilton on the telephone, please?

00:55:01.530 --> 00:55:03.970
Miss Cassaway, Mrs. Hamilton, on the phone, please.

00:55:04.050 --> 00:55:07.780
- Yes, Bishop. - Good morning, Gillian.

00:55:07.860 --> 00:55:09.090
Good morning, Dudley.

00:55:09.220 --> 00:55:12.130
I'll see you in a few minutes. I'll have to go out and see Matilda right now.

00:55:12.210 --> 00:55:14.760
- Bye, Debbie. - Bye, Dudley.

00:55:18.310 --> 00:55:25.620
- Thank you. - Oh, pretty.

00:55:27.810 --> 00:55:29.760
Are you expecting a letter?

00:55:30.090 --> 00:55:33.590
One never knows. But if I should get one, the stamp will be worth saving.

00:55:33.720 --> 00:55:36.280
I'll have Mrs. Hamilton in a moment, sir.

00:55:36.370 --> 00:55:38.970
- Are you going to see Mrs. Hamilton today? - I hope to.

00:55:39.170 --> 00:55:40.530
May I come along? I'd like to meet her.

00:55:41.080 --> 00:55:42.970
Mrs. Hamilton? Bishop Brougham.

00:55:45.830 --> 00:55:50.280
Hello, Mrs. Hamilton. How are you? I'm glad to hear it.

00:55:50.920 --> 00:55:55.060
Mrs. Hamilton, I'd like to see you today or this afternoon, if possible.

00:55:56.360 --> 00:55:58.100
Yes, it is. It's very urgent.

00:56:00.190 --> 00:56:04.380
- Oh, that's too bad. - Oh, that's terrible.

00:56:04.640 --> 00:56:07.560
You can. Oh, that's splendid.

00:56:07.830 --> 00:56:10.080
Thank you so much. I'll be there.

00:56:10.180 --> 00:56:13.280
Five o'clock this evening. Thank you, Mrs. Hamilton. Goodbye.

00:56:13.870 --> 00:56:16.960
Henry, you didn't make an appointment for this afternoon, did you?

00:56:17.040 --> 00:56:18.770
Well, yes, it was the only possible time, darling.

00:56:18.820 --> 00:56:22.700
But you can't do this to Reverend Miller while that rehearsal's being held just for you.

00:56:22.780 --> 00:56:26.460
Well, they'll get along all right without me, and other things more important.

00:56:26.630 --> 00:56:28.400
Besides, Mr. Miller will be delighted to see you.

00:56:28.480 --> 00:56:30.000
But it isn't the same, Henry. You're his brother.

00:56:30.060 --> 00:56:50.390
Bishop, I don't like going alone.

00:56:50.470 --> 00:56:53.880
It's the big house at the end of this street, driver.

00:56:53.960 --> 00:56:59.830
Dudley, I take it that you have the money for the taxi?

00:57:00.410 --> 00:57:06.770
- No. What makes you think I have money? - Oh, I just thought that you being an angel...

00:57:06.770 --> 00:57:08.770
Oh, goodness!

00:57:08.770 --> 00:57:15.010
- I'm sorry. - I'm sorry. I'm sorry.

00:57:31.790 --> 00:57:34.170
I won't be very late. I may even join you before the end of the rehearsal.

00:57:34.250 --> 00:57:35.160
Please try.

00:57:35.290 --> 00:57:37.630
At any rate, I'll see you in front of St. Timothy's in exactly one hour.

00:57:37.710 --> 00:57:38.940
Try.

00:57:39.250 --> 00:57:43.090
I will be there. Good. This is it, driver.

00:58:05.340 --> 00:58:07.320
- Good evening, Bishop Rowan. - How are you, Stephens?

00:58:07.320 --> 00:58:09.320
Mrs. Hamilton is in the drawing room, sir.

00:58:09.320 --> 00:58:25.670
Thank you. Mrs. Hamilton, I've come to tell you.

00:58:25.670 --> 00:58:27.720
You've come to apologize, I trust.

00:58:27.720 --> 00:58:30.000
Exactly. Upon mature consideration,

00:58:30.000 --> 00:58:34.000
my objections seem petty indeed compared with the generosity of your gesture.

00:58:34.000 --> 00:58:35.920
I'm very much relieved. Sit down, Bishop.

00:58:35.920 --> 00:58:36.920
Thank you.

00:58:36.920 --> 00:58:40.160
What hurt me most was to think that my instinct betrayed me

00:58:40.160 --> 00:58:43.160
in recommending you for the position you now hold.

00:58:43.160 --> 00:58:45.150
I am unceasingly grateful, Mrs. Hamilton.

00:58:45.150 --> 00:58:48.150
Now, in planning the cathedral, I'm taking it for granted

00:58:48.150 --> 00:58:51.100
that the George B. Hamilton Memorial Chapel shall be located...

00:58:51.100 --> 00:58:53.100
Just where you specified, Mrs. Hamilton.

00:58:53.100 --> 00:58:56.200
Oh. You no longer feel the effect will be

00:58:56.200 --> 00:58:58.210
that the whole cathedral was built in my husband's honour.

00:58:58.210 --> 00:59:00.000
That was the phrase used in the heat of the cathedral.

00:59:00.000 --> 00:59:05.240
dispute. What really matters is that the cathedral will be built. Good. You know, I will not have

00:59:05.240 --> 00:59:09.750
his name in some horrid little brass plaque. Oh, no, it'll be incised in marble. Large letters are

00:59:09.750 --> 00:59:15.590
gilded. Now, that large window depicting St. George and the dragon. Yes. I should very much

00:59:15.590 --> 00:59:22.110
like that the countenance of St. George suggests my late husband. Yes. Who do you see as the dragon?

00:59:23.190 --> 00:59:30.000
Oh, any dragon. Now, let's get the blueprints. Mrs. Hamilton, as we're now in such complete agreement,

00:59:30.030 --> 00:59:32.830
Would you mind very much if we postpone the discussion of the details?

00:59:32.830 --> 00:59:34.910
'Cause Julia's waiting for me. It's St. Timothy's.

00:59:34.910 --> 00:59:38.370
Oh, very well. We can go over the plans when we send for the transfer of the funds.

00:59:38.370 --> 00:59:41.200
Thank you so much. And I'm so glad we've settled our differences.

00:59:46.440 --> 00:59:48.590
Is anything the matter?

00:59:48.590 --> 00:59:51.600
Well, I... It doesn't seem quite right, does it?

00:59:51.600 --> 00:59:52.630
Oh.

00:59:52.630 --> 00:59:54.940
But... Oh, Stevens.

00:59:54.940 --> 00:59:59.470
There's something wrong about the bishop's chair.

00:59:59.470 --> 01:00:00.000
Well, madam.

01:00:00.860 --> 01:00:02.640
It must be the new varnish.

01:00:02.640 --> 01:00:04.910
The finisher should have warned us.

01:00:04.910 --> 01:00:08.080
I do hope I'm not harming the chair.

01:00:08.080 --> 01:00:10.280
Oh, no, no, not at all.

01:00:10.280 --> 01:00:13.580
Send to a furniture shop, or plumbers, or turpentine.

01:00:13.580 --> 01:00:14.420
I'll do something, Steve.

01:00:14.420 --> 01:00:15.430
Yes, ma'am.

01:00:15.430 --> 01:00:16.230
Oh, dear.

01:00:16.230 --> 01:00:17.800
Oh.

01:00:17.800 --> 01:00:19.010
I wonder--

01:00:19.010 --> 01:00:20.830
Would you give it a little pull at the back?

01:00:20.830 --> 01:00:21.450
Yes.

01:00:21.450 --> 01:00:22.560
Yes, sir.

01:00:33.480 --> 01:00:38.700
It's been a long time since Henry's been down here.

01:00:38.700 --> 01:00:41.300
I wish he had come.

01:00:41.300 --> 01:00:45.610
You know, when he was here, he was so close to people,

01:00:45.610 --> 01:00:47.720
so loved by everybody.

01:00:47.720 --> 01:00:48.670
Uh-huh.

01:00:48.670 --> 01:00:49.670
And how does it seem to you now?

01:00:49.670 --> 01:00:51.910
That in dreaming of his cathedral,

01:00:51.910 --> 01:00:53.910
he's moved away from the people he loved?

01:00:53.910 --> 01:00:56.750
Yes.

01:00:56.750 --> 01:00:58.760
It's going to be such a disappointment for Reverend Miller not to see him.

01:00:58.760 --> 01:01:00.000
Oh, well, he doesn't have to.

01:01:00.440 --> 01:01:06.560
- Hello, Mr. Miller. - Oh, Mrs. Brolin, so good of you to come.

01:01:06.660 --> 01:01:08.560
Oh, not at all. I'm delighted to be here.

01:01:08.660 --> 01:01:10.560
This is Mr. Dudley, the Bishop's new assistant.

01:01:10.660 --> 01:01:12.560
- Mr. Dudley, a pleasure. - Thank you, Mr. Miller.

01:01:12.660 --> 01:01:15.560
Uh, the Bishop will try to get here later, Mr. Miller.

01:01:15.660 --> 01:01:19.930
- Something important came up. - Oh, of course. He's a busy man now.

01:01:20.030 --> 01:01:22.140
- Yes. - Uh, this is Mrs. Duffy.

01:01:22.240 --> 01:01:24.140
I know, Mrs. Duffy. How are you?

01:01:24.240 --> 01:01:27.140
- It's always an honor to have you here at St. Timothy's. - Oh, thank you.

01:01:27.240 --> 01:01:29.140
And this is Mr. Dudley.

01:01:29.240 --> 01:01:29.670
Absolutely.

01:01:30.060 --> 01:01:33.060
- Mrs. Duffy is the organist. - Oh, I'm sure Mrs. Duffy plays in chanting, lady.

01:01:34.970 --> 01:01:37.010
I'm afraid some of our boys are a little late.

01:01:37.010 --> 01:01:41.530
We really should begin, but I don't see how we can.

01:01:41.530 --> 01:01:43.530
It's really quite embarrassing.

01:01:43.530 --> 01:01:48.860
But you know, it is a little difficult to compete with basketball and Christmas.

01:01:48.860 --> 01:01:51.320
They're all good boys at heart.

01:01:51.320 --> 01:01:53.390
I know they are. They'll show up.

01:01:53.390 --> 01:01:54.530
I hope so.

01:01:54.530 --> 01:01:57.880
- Hello, Bobby. - Hello.

01:01:57.880 --> 01:02:00.000
What do you think?

01:02:00.280 --> 01:02:02.080
- I sing Bruce Soprano.

01:02:02.080 --> 01:02:03.080
- You good?

01:02:03.080 --> 01:02:04.290
- I don't know.

01:02:04.290 --> 01:02:05.930
- Well, how about giving out?

01:02:05.930 --> 01:02:06.980
- Me alone?

01:02:06.980 --> 01:02:08.330
- Well, you got George up there.

01:02:08.330 --> 01:02:09.180
Hello, George.

01:02:09.180 --> 01:02:10.360
- Hello.

01:02:10.360 --> 01:02:12.040
- Well, what do you say?

01:02:12.040 --> 01:02:13.920
- Okay.

01:02:13.920 --> 01:02:15.080
- You ready, Mrs. Duffy?

01:02:15.080 --> 01:02:15.920
- Oh, yes.

01:02:15.920 --> 01:02:17.540
- Hit her.

01:04:41.960 --> 01:04:45.400
That was beautiful, really beautiful

01:04:45.400 --> 01:04:50.270
You know, you've all grown up so since the bishop and I lived in this parish

01:04:50.270 --> 01:04:53.140
That I hardly recognized any of you

01:04:53.140 --> 01:04:55.610
But I'm so proud of you

01:04:55.610 --> 01:04:57.640
And I know he's going to be too

01:04:57.640 --> 01:04:59.540
Well, thank you

01:05:01.050 --> 01:05:07.560
- Mr. Miller, that was wonderful. - I can't thank you enough, Mrs. Brown, and you, Mr. Dudley.

01:05:07.560 --> 01:05:10.020
Oh, I am so thrilled the bishop couldn't have been here.

01:05:10.020 --> 01:05:16.640
- Are you, uh, are you all right? - Yes, yes, thank you.

01:05:16.640 --> 01:05:21.190
Whatever he's keeping, Stevens. Oh, Stevens, there you are.

01:05:21.190 --> 01:05:25.080
I'm terribly sorry, madam, but the furniture shop was closed until after New Year.

01:05:25.080 --> 01:05:28.810
I can't seem to find a plumber and wore a turpentine.

01:05:28.810 --> 01:05:30.000
This is preposterous.

01:05:30.000 --> 01:05:32.170
Would some witch hazel be of any use?

01:05:33.680 --> 01:05:36.560
- Mrs. Hamilton, might I use your telephone? - Yes, of course, it's over there.

01:05:36.640 --> 01:05:37.620
Thank you, sir.

01:05:37.620 --> 01:05:53.360
Tilda, this is Bishop Braum.

01:05:53.580 --> 01:05:55.090
I'm over at Mrs. Hamilton's.

01:05:55.170 --> 01:05:57.520
Will you bring me another pair of trousers at once, please?

01:06:00.380 --> 01:06:01.380
Well, what difference does it make?

01:06:01.380 --> 01:06:03.590
Just bring me another pair of trousers.

01:06:03.590 --> 01:06:06.300
Thank you.

01:06:06.300 --> 01:06:08.200
I'm so very sorry this has happened.

01:06:08.200 --> 01:06:12.130
Well, if only I could get in touch with Julia or Dudley.

01:06:12.130 --> 01:06:15.870
Now, now, don't be nervous, Bishop.

01:06:15.870 --> 01:06:16.800
Have a chair.

01:06:16.800 --> 01:06:18.820
Thank you.

01:06:18.820 --> 01:06:20.190
I have one.

01:06:20.190 --> 01:06:28.400
He isn't here.

01:06:28.400 --> 01:06:30.000
Well, perhaps to meet him with Mr.

01:06:30.000 --> 01:06:35.200
- Mrs. Hamilton was more important than he thought. - I suppose.

01:06:35.300 --> 01:06:42.080
- Better go on home. - You know, Dudley, it's a strange thing.

01:06:42.490 --> 01:06:46.720
- What's strange? - You seem to be able to make me feel as if...

01:06:46.900 --> 01:06:50.160
- ...everything's going to be all right. - Well, it could be if...

01:06:50.260 --> 01:06:55.090
- If what? - If people could only learn to behave like human beings.

01:06:58.240 --> 01:06:59.410
Hey, taxi!

01:07:07.360 --> 01:07:12.310
Here's the cab, Julia. Please wait, Sylvester.

01:07:12.980 --> 01:07:14.560
Okay, sure, Mr. I'll wait.

01:07:15.300 --> 01:07:16.140
Sylvester!

01:07:23.990 --> 01:07:26.610
Good evening. If you wait just a moment, I'll be right with you.

01:07:27.000 --> 01:07:30.000
Now, this is one of our most exclusive models.

01:07:30.440 --> 01:07:34.440
Oh, that's lovely, just simply ravishing, so chic, so yummy.

01:07:34.440 --> 01:07:37.210
- It is sweet, isn't it? - Oh, it's stunning.

01:07:37.210 --> 01:07:39.380
Not everyone could wear such a daring hat.

01:07:39.380 --> 01:07:43.540
But, of course, it was made from a dad.

01:08:00.000 --> 01:08:10.250
don't you know my name was sylvester uh it's up there on your card oh sylvester could you drive

01:08:10.250 --> 01:08:14.050
us through the park please oh that's way out of your way oh now what's the matter with you

01:08:14.050 --> 01:08:19.660
sylvester are you getting bored with us oh no i'll drive you by way of mexico city if you want me

01:08:19.660 --> 01:08:30.000
thank you thirdly i'm having so much fun are you julia yes are you really yes i feel as if i were doing

01:08:30.000 --> 01:08:37.740
something wicked. Why? I don't know. Somehow it seems wrong to have so much fun, but I can't figure out

01:08:37.740 --> 01:08:43.880
what's wrong about it. You folks happen to know what the main trouble with this country is? Oh, I've

01:08:43.880 --> 01:08:51.270
heard several versions of that. Do you know, Sylvester? Well, I think I do. The main trouble is there are too

01:08:51.270 --> 01:08:56.300
many people who don't know where they're going and they want to get there too fast. I'll take you two.

01:08:56.860 --> 01:08:59.600
I'd call you unusual. Thank you.

01:09:00.090 --> 01:09:01.590
You're very perceptive.

01:09:01.590 --> 01:09:03.420
First place, you know your destination.

01:09:03.420 --> 01:09:05.470
But you're in no hurry to get there.

01:09:05.470 --> 01:09:08.330
You want to enjoy some scenery en route.

01:09:08.330 --> 01:09:13.600
And you're not reluctant to spend an extra four bits for a day tour with Mother Nature.

01:09:14.360 --> 01:09:16.220
Hey, look where you're going!

01:09:16.220 --> 01:09:20.810
Well, well, my goodness.

01:09:20.810 --> 01:09:23.390
Did you see the way I missed that truck?

01:09:23.390 --> 01:09:25.600
Well, it was just like a miracle.

01:09:25.600 --> 01:09:27.920
Yes, but don't overplay your hand.

01:09:40.560 --> 01:09:44.040
Hey, Sylvester, pull up here. Come on, Julia, we're going skating.

01:09:44.040 --> 01:09:47.550
Oh, no, no, we mustn't. It's late. We couldn't. Do you think we could?

01:09:47.550 --> 01:09:49.890
Well, if Henry's waited this long, he can wait a little longer.

01:09:49.890 --> 01:09:54.000
- Yeah. - Sylvester, stop the car.

01:09:54.000 --> 01:10:00.000
- I am wicked. - Well, if you are,

01:10:00.060 --> 01:10:01.940
Well, so am I. That's impossible.

01:10:02.020 --> 01:10:05.940
- Can you skate, Sylvester? - Oh, I used to when I was a boy, but I'm too old now.

01:10:06.020 --> 01:10:10.340
Come on, pull on some skates. You'll find out how young you are.

01:10:30.480 --> 01:10:52.120
I'm so sure of that.

01:10:52.120 --> 01:10:54.040
That's right.

01:11:05.820 --> 01:11:06.530
Pretty head.

01:11:53.580 --> 01:11:55.230
God, leave us as heaven.

01:11:55.230 --> 01:11:57.400
You found the perfect word.

01:12:23.800 --> 01:12:26.530
You're a beautiful skater, Juliana. Thanks, you're beautiful.

01:12:47.530 --> 01:12:52.920
Oh, oh, oh!

01:13:05.280 --> 01:13:09.320
- Excuse me, dear. - Surely.

01:13:09.400 --> 01:13:12.240
Keep cool, Sylvester. Keep cool.

01:13:12.320 --> 01:13:22.400
- The other one. - That's it.

01:13:22.480 --> 01:13:25.350
- All right, now relax. - No, no, not collapse, just relax.

01:13:28.480 --> 01:13:30.000
Hang on.

01:13:30.090 --> 01:13:32.080
Help me now.

01:14:31.180 --> 01:14:32.120
Thank you.

01:15:01.790 --> 01:15:05.290
- Thank you. - How much do I owe you, Sylvester?

01:15:05.390 --> 01:15:07.130
Not a cent, my friend.

01:15:07.280 --> 01:15:10.710
My pockets are just bulging with the coins of self-satisfaction.

01:15:10.930 --> 01:15:13.110
- Do you want to know why? - I'd love to know.

01:15:13.250 --> 01:15:16.590
Because you and the little lady have restored my faith in human nature.

01:15:16.670 --> 01:15:19.650
That's why. Good night, Dudley. Good night, Julia.

01:15:19.750 --> 01:15:22.320
- Good night, Sylvester. - Good night, Sylvester.

01:15:24.870 --> 01:15:30.000
Sylvester is a noble soul.

01:15:30.350 --> 01:15:34.000
that his children's children will rise up and call him blessed.

01:15:34.790 --> 01:15:37.880
Dudley, this has been the most wonderful evening I've had in years.

01:15:38.890 --> 01:15:41.490
This has been the most wonderful evening I've had in centuries.

01:15:43.130 --> 01:15:45.010
I hope I haven't left the keyhole.

01:15:45.770 --> 01:15:47.170
Oh, I hear it. It's open.

01:15:49.330 --> 01:15:50.730
Well, hello, Queenie.

01:15:50.930 --> 01:15:52.810
Hello, Queenie, how are you? Can I help you?

01:15:53.010 --> 01:15:54.230
Yes, please.

01:15:54.660 --> 01:15:56.380
Oh, I forgot about you.

01:15:56.580 --> 01:15:57.400
Hello, Henry.

01:15:58.060 --> 01:16:00.000
Well, Henry, what happened to you?

01:16:00.060 --> 01:16:02.060
You thought you were going to meet us at St. Timothy's.

01:16:02.140 --> 01:16:04.060
- What happened to you? It's very late. - Take that.

01:16:04.140 --> 01:16:06.370
Thank you. You'll never guess.

01:16:06.940 --> 01:16:08.860
- We went skating. - Oh, come on.

01:16:08.940 --> 01:16:11.740
- There you are. - See you in a minute, Henry.

01:16:11.820 --> 01:16:15.030
- All right. - Skating.

01:16:15.110 --> 01:16:17.750
- Yes. Dudley's the most marvelous skater.

01:16:17.830 --> 01:16:20.000
He even made me imagine that I was good.

01:16:20.170 --> 01:16:22.250
Oh, Henry, you should have heard those boys sing at St. Timothy's.

01:16:22.330 --> 01:16:25.530
- He was absolutely heavenly. - I'm sure of that.

01:16:25.610 --> 01:16:28.250
- Did you have a successful meeting with Mrs. Hamilton? - Yes, did you?

01:16:28.330 --> 01:16:29.920
- Satisfactory? - Good.

01:16:30.120 --> 01:16:32.160
Oh, dear, I want to see Debbie before she goes to sleep.

01:16:33.370 --> 01:16:35.950
Oh, you haven't said a word about it.

01:16:36.130 --> 01:16:36.710
About what?

01:16:37.300 --> 01:16:38.900
My hat, my new hat.

01:16:42.980 --> 01:16:43.740
Oh, what do you think?

01:16:44.800 --> 01:16:45.200
Charming.

01:16:45.690 --> 01:16:46.710
Oh, thank you.

01:16:46.950 --> 01:16:47.690
I'll be right down.

01:16:48.440 --> 01:16:51.790
There's one thing I know about this.

01:16:51.910 --> 01:16:53.430
Julia is absolutely blameless.

01:16:53.550 --> 01:16:54.470
Of course she is.

01:16:54.590 --> 01:16:56.170
You deliberately stopped me from joining you.

01:16:56.620 --> 01:16:57.820
Julia had a very good time.

01:16:59.280 --> 01:17:00.000
Well, I didn't.

01:17:00.800 --> 01:17:05.080
If you'd sent me to represent you with Mrs. Hamilton, I would have gone.

01:17:05.180 --> 01:17:07.740
You didn't, so I represented you with your wife.

01:17:07.780 --> 01:17:16.400
Is that part of the normal duties of an... of an angel?

01:17:16.450 --> 01:17:21.380
Sometimes, Henry. Angels must rush in where fools fear to tread.

01:17:21.480 --> 01:17:24.360
I haven't the faintest idea what that means, and I don't want it explained to me.

01:17:24.390 --> 01:17:29.160
Dudley, you can go now.

01:17:30.060 --> 01:17:32.070
- I've solved my problem. - Have you?

01:17:32.170 --> 01:17:34.380
Mrs. Hamilton is giving the money for the cathedral.

01:17:35.480 --> 01:17:37.280
Well, that was a foregone conclusion.

01:17:37.380 --> 01:17:40.560
If you were willing to make a slight sacrifice of your principles.

01:17:41.010 --> 01:17:47.090
Don't you think it's worth it for this glorious edifice?

01:17:47.300 --> 01:17:50.590
I'm not so sure if it's glory at a time like this.

01:17:50.690 --> 01:17:53.940
- Oh, you're not? - No, Henry, I'm not.

01:17:54.150 --> 01:17:57.000
You know, these are lean years for the world.

01:17:57.100 --> 01:18:00.000
So many people need food. So many people need shelter.

01:18:00.890 --> 01:18:04.550
That, uh, that big roof could make so many little roofs.

01:18:04.630 --> 01:18:06.840
But we're dealing with a materialistic, selfish woman.

01:18:06.920 --> 01:18:08.450
She wouldn't listen to talk like that.

01:18:08.760 --> 01:18:13.800
- Did you try? - It's all arranged. It's finished.

01:18:14.460 --> 01:18:16.390
You came here so that I could have a cathedral.

01:18:16.470 --> 01:18:18.050
Well, I've got a cathedral, and now I want you to go.

01:18:18.130 --> 01:18:21.210
I want you to get out of my house and out of my life and away from Julia.

01:18:21.720 --> 01:18:23.170
Suppose you pray for that?

01:18:23.340 --> 01:18:25.090
It was your prayer they brought me here.

01:18:48.590 --> 01:18:51.480
- That was no prayer. - It was right from my heart.

01:18:51.560 --> 01:18:54.200
- I want you to go. - Julia doesn't.

01:18:54.200 --> 01:18:57.880
Julia, get out! Get out!

01:19:00.000 --> 01:19:02.000
Julia's just about ready to come down the stairs.

01:19:02.000 --> 01:19:03.500
Now, don't let her see you like that.

01:19:03.500 --> 01:19:05.080
Try to calm yourself, Henry.

01:19:05.080 --> 01:19:21.980
It's nothing. Come out and say goodnight to her.

01:19:21.980 --> 01:19:25.930
He's gone. How should I know?

01:19:25.930 --> 01:19:28.610
Well, why did he leave so suddenly?

01:19:28.610 --> 01:19:29.700
I got rid of him.

01:19:30.060 --> 01:19:31.760
I told him to go away. I fired him.

01:19:31.760 --> 01:19:33.020
Why?

01:19:33.020 --> 01:19:36.440
Because he's incompetent. He's no good at his job and I cannot stand the sight of him.

01:20:06.170 --> 01:20:08.760
I'm sure that Dudley's never coming back.

01:20:09.870 --> 01:20:12.180
Darling, you must never say never.

01:20:12.660 --> 01:20:13.720
But where is he?

01:20:14.570 --> 01:20:20.830
Come here, dear. Now listen to me.

01:20:22.500 --> 01:20:24.280
Dudley wouldn't leave us without saying a word.

01:20:25.360 --> 01:20:28.000
And besides, he promised me he was going to tell you about Santa Claus.

01:20:28.770 --> 01:20:30.000
He knows Santa Claus very well.

01:20:32.840 --> 01:20:37.550
It's almost Christmas Eve. Soon I'll have to go to bed.

01:20:37.550 --> 01:20:41.530
I told you, dear, he wouldn't leave us without saying a word, would he, Matilda?

01:20:41.530 --> 01:20:45.980
Oh, no, no, that wouldn't be like him, like Mr. Dudley.

01:20:45.980 --> 01:20:49.060
Come along, Phoebe. I'll get you ready for dinner.

01:20:49.060 --> 01:20:51.740
Would you come see me when you'll get back, Mommy?

01:20:51.740 --> 01:20:53.360
Well, of course, dear.

01:21:15.800 --> 01:21:17.600
Here is the list of your calls, Bishop.

01:21:17.720 --> 01:21:19.010
Ending at Mrs. Hamilton's.

01:21:19.010 --> 01:21:19.680
Thank you.

01:21:20.480 --> 01:21:21.680
Now, if you'll be good enough, Miss Cassaway,

01:21:21.780 --> 01:21:23.380
here's the manuscript to my Christmas sermon.

01:21:23.480 --> 01:21:26.160
I shall want the original and five carbons

01:21:26.260 --> 01:21:27.640
for issuing to the press.

01:21:27.880 --> 01:21:30.000
If you get the typing done before I come back, just leave the copy.

01:21:30.000 --> 01:21:34.900
I'm sorry to keep it so long on Christmas Eve.

01:21:34.900 --> 01:21:37.710
Of course, sir, I understand.

01:21:37.710 --> 01:21:39.750
It must be done.

01:21:39.750 --> 01:21:43.220
Henry, I'm ready to start out now.

01:21:43.220 --> 01:21:45.940
Well, we go first to the Trubshaws.

01:21:45.940 --> 01:21:50.510
Then we go to the Vandovers.

01:21:50.510 --> 01:21:53.100
Goodbye, Miss Cassoway.

01:22:05.980 --> 01:22:08.480
- Sylvester. - Hello, Julia.

01:22:08.580 --> 01:22:12.480
- Hello, what are you doing here? - I've been waiting for about an hour

01:22:12.580 --> 01:22:14.980
hoping to be another skating party and I didn't want to miss it.

01:22:15.080 --> 01:22:17.410
Where's Dudley? Oh, you've got a preacher with you.

01:22:17.670 --> 01:22:21.340
- Is this... - Oh, I know.

01:22:21.440 --> 01:22:24.160
There's going to be a wedding ceremony, you and Dudley.

01:22:24.740 --> 01:22:28.130
Sylvester, this is my husband, Bishop Brom.

01:22:28.280 --> 01:22:30.000
- How are you doing? - Oh.

01:22:31.500 --> 01:22:34.040
Oh, 247 North Maple.

01:22:39.790 --> 01:22:41.750
247 North Maple.

01:23:01.210 --> 01:23:05.990
Oh, why, Dudley, it's you. I, I didn't see, well, where have you been?

01:23:05.990 --> 01:23:10.030
All of us have been worrying about you, and poor Mrs. Brahm.

01:23:10.030 --> 01:23:11.270
What about Mrs. Brahm?

01:23:11.270 --> 01:23:16.270
Well, she's been popping in and out of here all day, asking have you seen Dudley, have you heard from him?

01:23:16.270 --> 01:23:17.420
Where is she, Mildred?

01:23:17.420 --> 01:23:21.560
She and the bishop had some calls to make, finishing at Mrs. Hamilton's.

01:23:21.560 --> 01:23:27.180
Let me type that sermon for you. Oh, no.

01:23:27.180 --> 01:23:29.070
You go on. Oh, no, the bishop told me.

01:23:29.070 --> 01:23:30.000
It's almost Christmas.

01:23:30.280 --> 01:23:34.550
- You must have shopping to do. - Come on, Mildred.

01:23:35.250 --> 01:23:36.250
Thank you, Dudley.

01:23:38.950 --> 01:23:43.600
- Merry Christmas, Mildred. - Merry Christmas, Dudley.

01:24:15.160 --> 01:24:18.120
Take a sermon.

01:24:18.120 --> 01:24:23.580
Tonight, I want to tell you the story of an empty stocking.

01:24:23.580 --> 01:24:27.750
Once upon a midnight clear, there was a child's cry.

01:24:27.750 --> 01:24:29.890
A blazing star hung over a stable.

01:24:30.220 --> 01:24:32.390
And wise men came with birthday gifts.

01:24:32.390 --> 01:24:35.600
Have you got that?

01:24:35.600 --> 01:24:37.820
We haven't forgotten that night down the centuries.

01:24:37.820 --> 01:24:39.820
We celebrated with stars hung on the Christmas tree

01:24:39.820 --> 01:24:44.080
and the cry of bells and gifts, especially with gifts.

01:24:44.080 --> 01:24:48.600
We buy them and wrap them and put them under the tree.

01:24:48.600 --> 01:24:52.300
You give me a tie, I give you a book.

01:24:52.300 --> 01:24:55.700
Aunt Martha always wanted an orange squeezer.

01:24:55.700 --> 01:24:57.680
Uncle Harry can use a new pipe.

01:25:01.530 --> 01:25:04.570
Oh, we forget nobody. Adult, a child.

01:25:04.570 --> 01:25:08.770
All the stockings are filled. All, that is, except one.

01:25:34.030 --> 01:25:38.080
- I'm sorry, Matilda. - Mr. Dudley, I knew you'd come back.

01:25:38.180 --> 01:25:41.980
- I knew you hadn't walked out on us. - Of course not.

01:25:42.040 --> 01:25:45.290
Debbie's been so worried, and as for Mrs. Brom...

01:25:45.390 --> 01:25:48.290
Well, upstairs, Matilda. Tell Debbie I'll see her later.

01:25:48.390 --> 01:25:50.900
- First, I have some work to do. - I'll tell her.

01:26:38.000 --> 01:26:43.170
- One moment, please. Is Mrs. Hamilton expecting you? - Not exactly, but she'll wish to see me.

01:26:43.170 --> 01:26:47.510
- I'm the bishop's assistant. - The bishop is expected, but not the assistant.

01:26:47.510 --> 01:26:56.720
- I told you, Stevens. She'll wish to see me. - Yes. Yes.

01:27:54.600 --> 01:27:59.350
.

01:28:21.150 --> 01:28:24.530
This was composed for you, my darling, and you only, Alan.

01:28:24.970 --> 01:28:26.650
When her husband's name was George.

01:28:28.420 --> 01:28:29.480
Mmm, mmm, mmm.

01:29:32.140 --> 01:29:32.660
Thank you.

01:30:31.820 --> 01:30:37.550
- Good evening. - Music, you're a pleasure.

01:30:37.630 --> 01:30:39.790
There's no one living but me that knows that composition.

01:30:39.800 --> 01:30:44.340
Yes, it's a shame that only you and I appreciate the lost genius of Alan Cartwright.

01:30:44.340 --> 01:30:47.700
- You know about Alan Cartwright? - Oh, yes.

01:30:47.850 --> 01:30:51.990
The world lost a brilliant young composer when he was... when he died.

01:30:52.130 --> 01:31:00.000
- It was nearly 40 years ago. - I'm much older than you think.

01:31:00.380 --> 01:31:01.670
Come, let us sit down.

01:31:01.830 --> 01:31:09.480
What is your name?

01:31:10.280 --> 01:31:11.160
My name is Dudley.

01:31:14.670 --> 01:31:17.030
But tell me about Anna and you.

01:31:23.240 --> 01:31:30.000
Alan Cartridge.

01:31:30.640 --> 01:31:40.340
was the only man i ever loved we were engaged to be married and i got frightened he had nothing and

01:31:40.340 --> 01:31:53.570
i was afraid of poverty he went away i never saw him i never loved george hamilton he was very much

01:31:53.570 --> 01:32:00.000
in love with me he was wealthy i've spent a fortune

01:32:00.000 --> 01:32:02.530
and honoring his memory in empty monuments.

01:32:08.490 --> 01:32:16.730
Well, the mansion never took a call here before.

01:32:16.810 --> 01:32:18.730
- What do I owe you now? - Oh, no charge, Bishop.

01:32:18.810 --> 01:32:20.890
- I got nothing better to do. - Thank you, Sylvester.

01:32:20.970 --> 01:32:22.210
I'll be seeing you, Julia.

01:32:31.210 --> 01:32:38.010
someone at the door that might be henry and julia oh bishop no i won't i i can't see them now oh yes

01:32:38.010 --> 01:32:47.170
you will no yes that's right agnes just go out and greet them in your usual warm-hearted manner

01:32:49.880 --> 01:32:54.920
the bishop and mrs brougham are calling madam yes

01:33:00.700 --> 01:33:03.200
Oh, your stay for dinner, Dudley?

01:33:03.250 --> 01:33:06.760
I'm afraid I can't, Agnes. I have a great deal of work to do.

01:33:07.120 --> 01:33:09.220
But don't keep Henry and Julia waiting.

01:33:12.710 --> 01:33:23.720
Julia. How do you do, Mrs. Hamilton?

01:33:25.120 --> 01:33:27.180
How nice of you to come and see me.

01:33:27.780 --> 01:33:29.120
And Henry.

01:33:30.030 --> 01:33:33.330
A Merry Christmas, a Merry Christmas.

01:33:33.330 --> 01:33:39.850
Come, let's go into the drawing room.

01:33:39.850 --> 01:33:41.950
- Henry? - Oh, yes, Mrs. Hampton.

01:33:41.950 --> 01:33:43.230
A Merry Christmas to you, too.

01:33:43.230 --> 01:33:45.270
Oh, come now, Henry, we're very old friends.

01:33:45.270 --> 01:33:48.350
You must call me Agnes.

01:33:48.350 --> 01:33:50.510
And you, too, Julia, dear.

01:33:50.510 --> 01:33:54.390
Oh, yes, yes, of course.

01:33:54.390 --> 01:33:58.630
Oh, but he's gone already.

01:33:58.630 --> 01:34:00.000
Who?

01:34:01.600 --> 01:34:04.040
- Dudley. - He was here?

01:34:04.140 --> 01:34:05.440
- Yes. - I should have known it.

01:34:05.540 --> 01:34:07.540
- Where did he go? - Oh, the poor man.

01:34:07.640 --> 01:34:09.440
He said he had so much work to do.

01:34:09.540 --> 01:34:11.540
Henry, you must make him take some rest.

01:34:11.640 --> 01:34:13.340
I've been trying to make him do just that.

01:34:13.440 --> 01:34:15.840
Oh, I can't thank you enough for sending him to me.

01:34:15.940 --> 01:34:17.340
Oh, but do sit down.

01:34:17.440 --> 01:34:22.630
You know, my dear, meeting Dudley has been the greatest spiritual experience of my life.

01:34:23.000 --> 01:34:26.400
- I'm so glad you've seen him. - How did you ever find him, Henry?

01:34:27.160 --> 01:34:29.190
- It was more or less of an accident. - It was a miracle.

01:34:29.300 --> 01:34:30.000
Oh, indeed it was.

01:34:30.220 --> 01:34:34.040
talking with this wonderful, understanding man who's, has...

01:34:34.040 --> 01:34:38.000
Henry, I've changed my mind about the cathedral.

01:34:38.000 --> 01:34:39.920
I'm going to give my money to those who need it.

01:34:39.920 --> 01:34:41.920
To the poor and the homeless

01:34:41.920 --> 01:34:45.920
and the unappreciated people here in the city and all over the world.

01:34:45.920 --> 01:34:49.810
And I want you to direct the spending of the money.

01:34:49.810 --> 01:34:51.670
Oh, now, Henry, you see what Dudley has done.

01:34:51.670 --> 01:34:53.670
- Yes, I see. - Now you understand.

01:34:53.670 --> 01:34:57.550
Well, thank you, Mrs. Hamilton.

01:34:57.550 --> 01:35:00.000
I'll be home later for dinner or something.

01:35:00.600 --> 01:35:03.590
I don't know what time. Good-bye.

01:35:51.280 --> 01:35:51.760
Hello, Professor.

01:35:52.680 --> 01:35:55.390
Henry, come in, my dear.

01:35:55.610 --> 01:35:56.070
Come in.

01:35:56.620 --> 01:35:57.880
Sit down, Henry.

01:35:58.450 --> 01:35:59.350
Let me take your coat.

01:35:59.550 --> 01:36:00.000
No, thanks.

01:36:00.090 --> 01:36:04.230
Oh, no, not there. Here, this is the only reliable chair.

01:36:04.350 --> 01:36:07.680
Well, this is a surprise and an honor.

01:36:08.080 --> 01:36:10.050
We must have a glass of sherry.

01:36:10.150 --> 01:36:12.260
Nothing. Ah, but I insist.

01:36:12.510 --> 01:36:16.320
I want to show you something. You see this bottle?

01:36:16.680 --> 01:36:19.400
You note that it is full. Now watch.

01:36:19.970 --> 01:36:22.590
This is something that even you can't explain

01:36:22.690 --> 01:36:25.090
with all your vast ecclesiastical knowledge.

01:36:25.090 --> 01:36:30.000
You will observe that it is still full.

01:36:30.000 --> 01:36:32.210
Now, how do you account for that?

01:36:32.330 --> 01:36:34.490
And, what's more, the sherry itself.

01:36:34.610 --> 01:36:37.670
It stimulates, it warms, it inspires,

01:36:37.790 --> 01:36:41.190
but no matter how much you drink, it never inebriates.

01:36:41.310 --> 01:36:44.110
I think I can account for it. Dudley's been here.

01:36:44.230 --> 01:36:46.860
Yes. And that bottle isn't all.

01:36:46.980 --> 01:36:49.580
He told me some things about history that opened my eyes.

01:36:49.700 --> 01:36:52.100
And today, I went up to the university library

01:36:52.220 --> 01:36:54.060
and I looked into some ancient texts,

01:36:54.180 --> 01:36:56.900
which no living scholar has been able to decipher.

01:36:57.020 --> 01:37:00.000
And suddenly, I found that I found

01:37:00.030 --> 01:37:06.870
could understand them and look this is what i've done thanks to dudley my history i'm actually

01:37:06.870 --> 01:37:16.360
writing it let's face it henry this dudley is no mortal man like the rest of us is he how did you

01:37:16.360 --> 01:37:23.390
know well i can't tell who is he what is he he says he's an angel an angel that's funny nothing

01:37:23.390 --> 01:37:28.800
stopped me from saying at that time from heaven that i'm not sure about an angel

01:37:30.350 --> 01:37:37.060
too bad he's such a nice fellow i suppose i should have known it nothing less than an angel

01:37:37.060 --> 01:37:41.570
could have put me to work i'm glad he's done some good for somebody he brought nothing but disaster

01:37:41.570 --> 01:37:47.180
for me that's absurd he and julia were in here the other day and she seemed happier than she's

01:37:47.180 --> 01:37:51.380
been in years in fact quite like her old delightful self she's a different person

01:37:51.380 --> 01:38:00.000
when she's with him he's made her despise me are you sure he has done that you think it's

01:38:00.000 --> 01:38:01.000
It's all my own fault.

01:38:01.000 --> 01:38:02.000
I didn't say that, Henry.

01:38:02.000 --> 01:38:05.050
This whole thing is a mystery beyond my powers of comprehension.

01:38:05.050 --> 01:38:07.840
Oh, I suppose I am to blame for everything.

01:38:07.840 --> 01:38:10.110
I asked for this in more ways than one.

01:38:10.110 --> 01:38:16.710
I suppose that Dudley came to me just to confirm that I'd already lost the love of Julia and Debbie.

01:38:16.710 --> 01:38:21.160
I've got a confession to make to you, my old friend.

01:38:21.160 --> 01:38:24.590
You sent me a coin that was very generous of you.

01:38:24.590 --> 01:38:26.670
And I was mean enough only to see its commercial value.

01:38:26.670 --> 01:38:28.850
Now it's gone. I don't know what's happened to it.

01:38:28.850 --> 01:38:30.000
Well, I do.

01:38:30.670 --> 01:38:33.240
Here it is.

01:38:33.240 --> 01:38:35.510
- Here it is. - Where did you find it?

01:38:35.510 --> 01:38:37.820
- Oh, don't tell me. - Yes.

01:38:37.820 --> 01:38:39.820
And what's more, he told me what it is.

01:38:39.820 --> 01:38:41.820
A museum piece, worth a fortune.

01:38:41.820 --> 01:38:43.900
No, I insist you keep it.

01:38:43.900 --> 01:38:45.720
Give it to Julia as my Christmas present.

01:38:45.720 --> 01:38:47.720
Who knows, it might bring luck to you both.

01:38:47.720 --> 01:38:49.930
You know, Henry, it seems strange.

01:38:49.930 --> 01:38:52.930
I mean, you being a bishop and I a broken-down old scholar.

01:38:52.930 --> 01:38:55.360
I feel terribly sorry for you.

01:38:55.360 --> 01:38:57.570
I wish there was something I could do to help.

01:38:57.570 --> 01:39:00.000
Well, thank you, but there's nothing to be done.

01:39:00.480 --> 01:39:04.570
There must be. You and Julia love each other. You always have.

01:39:04.750 --> 01:39:07.490
That's only partially true. I love Julia.

01:39:07.590 --> 01:39:09.040
Then why don't you fight for her?

01:39:09.140 --> 01:39:10.760
Fight? How can I fight against her?

01:39:10.860 --> 01:39:13.060
But you have a tremendous advantage over him.

01:39:13.160 --> 01:39:14.620
Advantage? Over an angel?

01:39:14.720 --> 01:39:19.550
That's precisely it. He's an angel. Julia is a creature of Earth.

01:39:19.650 --> 01:39:22.720
She's a woman, Henry. And you are a man.

01:39:34.380 --> 01:39:35.820
Isn't it beautiful?

01:39:35.820 --> 01:39:39.690
And he did every bit of it himself, and so quick, too.

01:39:39.690 --> 01:39:42.850
When I saw it, I couldn't believe my eyes.

01:39:42.850 --> 01:39:44.750
What a blessing he's been to us.

01:39:44.750 --> 01:39:47.380
The tree is lovely, Matilda.

01:39:47.380 --> 01:39:48.780
I'm glad you like it.

01:39:48.780 --> 01:39:50.360
It's been years since I've had a chance

01:39:50.360 --> 01:39:51.970
to work on the Christmas tree.

01:39:51.970 --> 01:39:57.550
I usually get the more disagreeable jobs.

01:39:57.550 --> 01:39:59.280
Good night, Matilda.

01:40:00.000 --> 01:40:13.070
- Sweet dreams. - Thank you, Mr. Dudley.

01:40:14.210 --> 01:40:16.340
I think my work here is almost finished.

01:40:16.420 --> 01:40:24.470
- I'll have to be moving along. - Where will he be going, Dudley?

01:40:24.550 --> 01:40:30.000
- Wherever they send me. - Who are they?

01:40:30.280 --> 01:40:33.890
Will we ever see you again?

01:40:33.890 --> 01:40:35.610
Mm-hmm.

01:40:35.610 --> 01:40:39.000
They seldom send us to the same place twice.

01:40:39.000 --> 01:40:41.140
We might form attachments.

01:40:41.140 --> 01:40:44.940
I don't know what you're talking about, Dudley.

01:40:44.940 --> 01:40:46.720
Of course not.

01:40:46.720 --> 01:40:53.180
Julia, I don't want to leave.

01:40:53.180 --> 01:40:58.690
There are few people who know the secret of making a heaven here on Earth.

01:40:58.690 --> 01:41:00.000
You are one of them.

01:41:00.280 --> 01:41:09.180
- You think you ought to go? - No, please, Julia. Don't send me away.

01:41:09.260 --> 01:41:15.520
- What are you saying, Dudley? - I'm tired of being a wanderer.

01:41:15.600 --> 01:41:21.890
I'm tired of an existence where one is neither hot nor cold, hungry nor full.

01:41:21.970 --> 01:41:28.930
Well, you must go away.

01:41:30.350 --> 01:41:54.490
- Come back. - Julia, I've never before had to fight an angel,

01:41:54.610 --> 01:41:57.240
but I suggest you take off your coat and put up your dukes.

01:41:57.460 --> 01:41:59.730
Now, why do you want to fight me, Henry?

01:42:02.200 --> 01:42:06.810
Because you're a thief, trying to steal my wife, my child, my all, the love that belongs to me.

01:42:07.080 --> 01:42:11.930
Don't you realize, Henry, that as an angel, I could quite possibly destroy you with a bolt of lightning.

01:42:12.130 --> 01:42:14.450
I don't care. Julia means more to me than my life.

01:42:14.870 --> 01:42:15.870
I'm not going to lose her.

01:42:18.640 --> 01:42:22.110
Then I have news for you. I'm going.

01:42:24.190 --> 01:42:28.010
I'll accept that as a fact when I see it happen.

01:42:28.990 --> 01:42:29.710
Oh, no, you won't.

01:42:30.280 --> 01:42:34.540
Because when I'm gone, you will never know that an angel visited your house.

01:42:35.340 --> 01:42:36.420
And Julia, what about her?

01:42:36.890 --> 01:42:38.730
There will be no memory with her either.

01:42:39.270 --> 01:42:42.070
Or with Debbie, or the professor, or anyone else.

01:42:43.530 --> 01:42:45.540
I don't trust you, Dudley.

01:42:45.850 --> 01:42:48.980
You may, Henry, because your prayer has been answered.

01:42:50.000 --> 01:42:52.600
That's not true. My prayer has not been answered. I was praying for a cathedral.

01:42:53.670 --> 01:42:56.100
No, Henry. You were praying for guidance.

01:42:56.320 --> 01:42:58.230
That has been given to you.

01:43:05.920 --> 01:43:06.760
Just a minute, please.

01:43:08.600 --> 01:43:09.650
Goodbye, Henry.

01:43:16.750 --> 01:43:20.300
Dudley, if we should need you again, will you come back?

01:43:21.180 --> 01:43:21.880
Not I.

01:43:22.300 --> 01:43:25.190
I should ask to be assigned to the other end of the universe.

01:43:26.190 --> 01:43:27.500
Is that because I was so difficult?

01:43:29.300 --> 01:43:30.000
This difficult.

01:43:30.250 --> 01:43:31.420
It was in me.

01:43:31.500 --> 01:43:35.220
When an immortal finds himself envying the mortal entrusted to his care,

01:43:35.300 --> 01:43:37.600
it's a danger signal.

01:43:37.680 --> 01:43:41.270
Take her in your arms and hold her tight.

01:43:41.350 --> 01:43:45.810
Come here. Kiss her for me, you lucky Henry.

01:44:26.620 --> 01:44:27.140
Julia!

01:44:34.600 --> 01:44:37.200
- Julia. - Shh.

01:44:39.240 --> 01:44:50.290
- Are you all right? - Did you get that for Debbie?

01:45:10.910 --> 01:45:18.470
what is it I don't know I just have the most inexplicable feeling of happiness

01:45:18.470 --> 01:45:24.740
oh you know something what downstairs in the kitchen is a big bowl of cider yes

01:45:24.740 --> 01:45:29.060
that's for tomorrow afternoon let's drink it now let's drink to us to our

01:45:29.060 --> 01:45:30.000
happiness into what lies

01:45:30.000 --> 01:45:32.580
ahead, and then let's smash the glasses in the fireplace.

01:45:37.350 --> 01:45:40.490
I'm coming from St. Timothy's.

01:45:41.200 --> 01:45:45.250
That cider's going to have to wait if you're going to give your Christmas Eve sermon.

01:45:45.910 --> 01:45:47.100
For my sermon.

01:45:47.770 --> 01:45:49.520
But that's better still.

01:46:34.860 --> 01:46:38.710
Tonight, I want to tell you the story of an empty stocking.

01:46:41.670 --> 01:46:48.680
Once upon a midnight clear, there was a child's cry.

01:46:48.980 --> 01:46:51.160
A blazing star hung over a stable,

01:46:52.560 --> 01:46:55.940
and wise men came with birthday gifts.

01:46:57.090 --> 01:46:59.480
We haven't forgotten that night down the centuries.

01:47:00.320 --> 01:47:02.820
We celebrate it with stars on Christmas trees,

01:47:03.450 --> 01:47:06.150
with the sound of bells and with gifts.

01:47:06.930 --> 01:47:08.550
But especially with gifts.

01:47:09.660 --> 01:47:13.590
You give me a book, I give you a tie.

01:47:14.630 --> 01:47:17.190
Aunt Martha has always wanted an orange squeeze.

01:47:17.650 --> 01:47:22.090
And Uncle Henry could do with a new pipe.

01:47:22.760 --> 01:47:25.760
We forget nobody, adult or child.

01:47:26.540 --> 01:47:28.040
All the stockings are filled.

01:47:28.920 --> 01:47:29.860
All that is...

01:47:30.830 --> 01:47:35.810
except one, and we have even forgotten to hang it up.

01:47:35.860 --> 01:47:39.470
A stocking for the child born in a manger.

01:47:39.570 --> 01:47:42.580
It's his birthday we're celebrating.

01:47:42.810 --> 01:47:45.240
Don't let us ever forget that.

01:47:45.950 --> 01:47:51.330
Let us ask ourselves what he would wish for most,

01:47:51.430 --> 01:47:55.090
and then let each put in his share.

01:47:55.090 --> 01:48:00.000
Loving kindness, warm heart.

01:48:01.720 --> 01:48:05.420
and the stretched out hand of tolerance.

01:48:05.420 --> 01:48:08.610
All the shining gifts make peace on earth.
